\id 1KI - Berean Standard Bible
\h 1 Kings
\toc2 1 Kings
\toc1 1 Kings
\mt1 1 Kings
\c 1
\s1 Abishag Cares for David
\m
\v 1 Now \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* was old and well along in years, and though they covered him with blankets, he could not keep warm.
\v 2 So his servants said to him, “Let us search for a young virgin for \zwd our lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, to attend to him and care for him and lie by his side to keep him warm.”
\b
\m
\v 3 Then they searched throughout Israel for a beautiful girl, and they found \zwd Abishag the Shunammite|Q321469\zwd* and brought her to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*.
\v 4 \zwd The girl|Q321469\zwd* was unsurpassed in beauty; she cared for \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and served him, but he had no relations with her.
\s1 Adonijah Usurps the Kingdom
\b
\m
\v 5 At that time \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd*, \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s son by \zwd Haggith|id="Q5638717" t="2"\zwd*, began to exalt himself, saying, “I will be king!” And he acquired chariots and horsemen and fifty men to run ahead of him.
\b
\m
\v 6 (\zwd His father|Q41370\zwd* had never once reprimanded him by saying, “Why do you act this way?” \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* was also very handsome, born next after Absalom.)
\b
\m
\v 7 So \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* conferred with \zwd Joab son of \zwd Zeruiah|\zwd*|Q1141064\zwd* and with \zwd Abiathar the priest|Q321804\zwd*, who supported him.
\v 8 But \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd*, \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*, \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd*, \zwd Shimei|\zwd*, \zwd Rei|\zwd*, and \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s mighty men would not join \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd*.
\b
\m
\v 9 \zdate|Q37085:P39:Q43459871:P580\*And \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* sacrificed sheep, oxen, and fattened calves near the stone of Zoheleth,\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Or the Serpent’s Stone\f* which is next to En-rogel. He invited all \zwd his royal brothers|\zwd* and all the men of Judah who were servants of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*.
\v 10 But he did not invite \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd*, \zwd Benaiah|Q816667\zwd*, \zwd the mighty men|\zwd*, or \zwd his brother Solomon|Q37085\zwd*.
\s1 Nathan and Bathsheba before David
\b
\m
\v 11 Then \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd* said to \zwd Bathsheba the mother of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*|Q272277\zwd*, “Have you not heard that \zwd Adonijah son of \zwd Haggith|id="Q5638717" t="2"\zwd*|Q360378\zwd* has become king, and \zwd our lord David|Q41370\zwd* does not know it?
\v 12 Now please, come and let me advise you. Save your own life and the life of \zwd your son Solomon|Q37085\zwd*.
\v 13 Go at once to \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* and say, ‘\zwd My lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, did you not swear to \zwd your maidservant|Q272277\zwd*, “Surely \zwd your son Solomon|Q37085\zwd* will reign after me, and he will sit on my throne”? Why then has \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* become king?’
\v 14 Then, while you are still there speaking with \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, I will come in after you and confirm your words.”
\b
\m
\v 15 So \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd* went to see \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* in his bedroom. Since \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* was very old, \zwd Abishag the Shunammite|Q321469\zwd* was serving him.
\v 16 And \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd* bowed down in homage to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, who asked, “What is your desire?”
\b
\m
\v 17 “My lord,” she replied, “you yourself swore to \zwd your maidservant|Q272277\zwd* by the LORD your God: ‘Surely \zwd your son Solomon|Q37085\zwd* will reign after me, and he will sit on my throne.’
\v 18 But now, behold, \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* has become king, and you, \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, do not know it.
\v 19 And he has sacrificed an abundance of oxen, fattened calves, and sheep, and has invited all the other sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, as well as \zwd Abiathar the priest|Q321804\zwd* and \zwd Joab the commander of the army|Q1141064\zwd*. But he has not invited \zwd your servant Solomon|Q37085\zwd*.
\v 20 And as for you, \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, the eyes of all Israel are upon you to tell them who will sit on the throne of \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* after him.
\v 21 Otherwise, when \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* rests with his fathers, I and \zwd my son Solomon|Q37085\zwd* will be counted as criminals.”
\b
\m
\v 22 And just then, while \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd* was still speaking with \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd* arrived.
\v 23 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* was told, “\zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd* is here.” And \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd* went in and bowed facedown before \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*.
\b
\m
\v 24 “\zwd My lord the king|Q41370\zwd*,” said \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd*, “did you say, ‘\zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* will reign after me, and he will sit on my throne’?
\v 25 For today he has gone down and sacrificed an abundance of oxen, fattened calves, and sheep, and has invited all the sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, the commanders \f + \fr 1:25 \ft Hebrew; LXX Joab the commander\f* of the army, and \zwd Abiathar the priest|Q321804\zwd*. And behold, they are eating and drinking before him, saying, ‘Long live \zwd King Adonijah|Q360378\zwd*!’
\v 26 But me your servant he has not invited, nor \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd*, nor \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*, nor \zwd your servant Solomon|Q37085\zwd*.
\v 27 Has \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* let this happen without informing \zwd your servant|Q319116\zwd* who should sit on the throne after \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*?”
\s1 David Renews His Oath to Bathsheba
\b
\m
\v 28 Then \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* said, “Call in \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd* for me.” So she came into \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s presence and stood before him.
\b
\m
\v 29 And \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* swore an oath, saying, “As surely as the LORD lives, who has redeemed my life from all distress,
\v 30 I will carry out this very day exactly what I swore to you by the LORD, the God of Israel: Surely \zwd your son Solomon|Q37085\zwd* will reign after me, and he will sit on my throne in my place.”
\b
\m
\v 31 \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd* bowed facedown in homage to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and said, “May \zwd my lord King David|Q41370\zwd* live forever!”
\s1 Solomon Anointed King
\r (1 Chronicles 29:21–25)
\b
\m
\v 32 Then \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* said, “Call in for me \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd*, \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd*, and \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*.” So they came before \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*.
\b
\m
\v 33 “Take my servants with you,” said \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. “Set \zwd my son Solomon|Q37085\zwd* on my own mule and take him down to Gihon.
\v 34 There \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd* are to anoint him king over Israel. You are to blow the ram’s horn and declare, ‘Long live \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*!’
\v 35 Then you shall go up with him, and he is to come and sit on my throne and reign in my place. For I have appointed him ruler over Israel and Judah.”
\b
\m
\v 36 “Amen,” replied \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*. “May the LORD, the God of \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, so declare it.
\v 37 Just as the LORD was with \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, so may He be with \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* and make his throne even greater than that of \zwd my lord King David|Q41370\zwd*.”
\b
\m
\v 38 Then \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd*, \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd*, and \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*, along with the Cherethites and Pelethites, went down and set \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* on \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd*’s mule, and they escorted him to Gihon.
\v 39 \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd* took the horn of oil from the tabernacle and anointed \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*. Then they blew the ram’s horn, and all the people proclaimed, “Long live \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*!”
\b
\m
\v 40 All the people followed him, playing flutes and rejoicing with such a great joy that the earth was split by the sound.
\s1 Adonijah Learns of Solomon’s Kingship
\b
\m
\v 41 Now \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* and all his guests were finishing their feast when they heard the sound of the ram’s horn. “Why is the city in such a loud uproar?” asked \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*.
\b
\m
\v 42 As he was speaking, suddenly \zwd Jonathan the son of \zwd Abiathar the priest|id="Q321804" t="2"\zwd*|Q20502598\zwd* arrived. “Come in,” said \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd*, “for you are a man of valor. You must be bringing good news.”
\b
\m
\v 43 “Not at all,” \zwd Jonathan|Q20502598\zwd* replied. “\zwd Our lord King David|Q41370\zwd* has made \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* king.
\v 44 And with \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* has sent \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd*, \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd*, and \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*, along with the Cherethites and Pelethites, and they have set him on \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s mule.
\v 45 \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd* have anointed him king at Gihon, and they have gone up from there with rejoicing that rings out in the city. That is the noise you hear.
\b
\m
\v 46 Moreover, \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* has taken his seat on the royal throne.
\b
\m
\v 47 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd*’s servants have also gone to congratulate \zwd our lord King David|Q41370\zwd*, saying, ‘May your God make the name of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* more famous than your own name, and may He make his throne greater than your throne.’
\b
\m And \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* has bowed in worship on his bed,
\v 48 saying, ‘Blessed be the LORD, the God of Israel! Today He has provided one \f + \fr 1:48 \ft LXX one of my offspring\f* to sit on my throne, and my eyes have seen it.’”
\b
\m
\v 49 At this, all the guests of \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* arose in terror and scattered.
\v 50 But \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd*, in fear of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, got up and went to take hold of the horns of the altar.
\b
\m
\v 51 It was reported to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*: “Behold, \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* fears \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, and he has taken hold of the horns of the altar, saying, ‘Let \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* first swear to me not to put \zwd his servant|Q360378\zwd* to the sword.’”
\b
\m
\v 52 And \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* replied, “If he is a man of character, not a single hair of his will fall to the ground. But if evil is found in him, he will die.”
\b
\m
\v 53 So \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* summoned \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* down from the altar, and he came and bowed down before \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, who said to him, “Go to your home.”
\c 2
\s1 David Instructs Solomon
\r (Psalms 37:1–40)
\m 
\v 1 As the time drew near for \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to die, he charged \zwd his son Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, 
\v 2 “I am about to go the way of all the earth. So be strong and prove yourself a man. 
\v 3 And keep the charge of the LORD your God to walk in His ways and to keep His statutes, commandments, ordinances, and decrees, as written in the Law of \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd*, so that you may prosper in all you do and wherever you turn, 
\v 4 and so that the LORD may fulfill His promise to me: ‘If your descendants take heed to walk faithfully before Me with all their heart and soul, you will never fail to have a man on the throne of Israel.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Moreover, you know what \zwd Joab son of \zwd Zeruiah|\zwd*|Q1141064\zwd* did to me—what he did to \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|\zwd*|\zwd* and \zwd Amasa son of \zwd Jether|\zwd*|\zwd*,\f + \fr 2:5 \ft Jether is a variant of Ithra; also in verse 32; see 2 Samuel 17:25.\f* the two commanders of the armies of Israel. He killed them in peacetime to avenge the blood of war. He stained with the blood of war the belt around his waist and the sandals on his feet.\f + \fr 2:5 \ft Hebrew; LXX He stained with innocent blood the belt around my waist and the sandals on my feet.\f* 
\v 6 So act according to your wisdom, and do not let his gray head go down to Sheol in peace. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 But show loving devotion \f + \fr 2:7 \ft Forms of the Hebrew chesed are translated here and in most cases throughout the Scriptures as loving devotion; the range of meaning includes love, goodness, kindness, faithfulness, and mercy, as well as loyalty to a covenant.\f* to the sons of \zwd Barzillai the Gileadite|id="Q6710215" t="2"\zwd*, and let them be among those who eat at your table, because they stood by me when I fled from \zwd your brother Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Keep an eye on \zwd Shimei the son of \zwd Gera|\zwd*|\zwd*, the Benjamite from Bahurim who is with you. He called down bitter curses against me on the day I went to Mahanaim, but when he came down to meet me at the Jordan, I swore to him by the LORD: ‘I will never put you to the sword.’ 
\v 9 Now therefore, do not hold him guiltless, for you are a wise man. You know what you ought to do to him to bring his gray head down to Sheol in blood.” 
\s1 David’s Reign and Death
\r (1 Chronicles 29:26–30)
\b
\m 
\v 10 \zdate|Q41370:P570\*Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* rested with his fathers and was buried in the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 11 The length of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s reign over Israel was forty years—seven years in Hebron and thirty-three years in Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* sat on the throne of \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, and his kingdom was firmly established. 
\s1 The Execution of Adonijah
\b
\m 
\v 13 Now \zwd Adonijah son of \zwd Haggith|id="Q5638717" t="2"\zwd*|Q360378\zwd* went to \zwd Bathsheba the mother of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*|Q272277\zwd*, and she asked, “Do you come in peace?” 
\b
\m “Yes, in peace,” he replied. 
\v 14 Then he said, “I have something to tell you.” 
\b
\m “Say it,” she answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 “You know that the kingship was mine,” he said. “All Israel expected that I should reign, but the kingship has turned to \zwd my brother|Q37085\zwd*, for it has come to him from the LORD. 
\v 16 So now I have just one request of you; do not deny me.” 
\b
\m “State your request,” she told him. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* replied, “Please speak to \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, since he will not turn you down. Let him give me \zwd Abishag the Shunammite|Q321469\zwd* as my wife.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 “Very well,” \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd* replied. “I will speak to \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* for you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 So \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd* went to \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* to speak to him for \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd*. \zwd The king|Q37085\zwd* stood up to greet her, bowed to her, and sat down on his throne. Then \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* had a throne brought for \zwd his mother|Q272277\zwd*, who sat down at his right hand. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 “I have just one small request of you,” she said. “Do not deny me.” 
\b
\m “Make your request, \zwd my mother|Q272277\zwd*,” \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* replied, “for I will not deny you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd* said, “Let \zwd Abishag the Shunammite|Q321469\zwd* be given to \zwd your brother Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* as his wife.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* answered \zwd his mother|Q272277\zwd*, “Why do you request \zwd Abishag the Shunammite|Q321469\zwd* for \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd*? Since he is my older brother, you might as well request the kingdom for him and for \zwd Abiathar the priest|Q321804\zwd* and for \zwd Joab son of \zwd Zeruiah|\zwd*|Q1141064\zwd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Then \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* swore by the LORD: “May God punish me, and ever so severely, if \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* has not made this request at the expense of his life. 
\v 24 And now, as surely as the LORD lives—the One who established me, who set me on the throne of \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, and who founded for me a dynasty as He promised—surely \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* shall be put to death today!” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 So \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* gave orders  to \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*, and he struck down \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* and he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 Then the king said to \zwd Abiathar the priest|Q321804\zwd*, “Go back to your fields in Anathoth. Even though you deserve to die, I will not put you to death at this time, since you carried the ark of the Lord GOD before \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, and you suffered through all that \zwd my father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* suffered.” 
\v 27 So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* banished \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* from the priesthood of the LORD and thus fulfilled the word that the LORD had spoken at Shiloh against the house of \zwd Eli|id="Q362021" t="2"\zwd*. 
\s1 The Execution of Joab
\b
\m 
\v 28 \zdate|Q1141064:P570\*When the news reached \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, who had conspired with \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd* but not with Absalom, he fled to the tent of the LORD and took hold of the horns of the altar. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 It was reported to \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*: “\zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* has fled to the tent of the LORD and is now beside the altar.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* sent \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*, saying, “Go, strike him down!” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 And \zwd Benaiah|Q816667\zwd* entered the tent of the LORD and said to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, “\zwd The king|Q37085\zwd* says, ‘Come out!’” 
\b
\m But \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* replied, “No, I will die here.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Benaiah|Q816667\zwd* relayed the message to \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd*, saying, “This is how \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* answered me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 And \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* replied, “Do just as he says. Strike him down and bury him, and so remove from me and from the house of \zwd my father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* the innocent blood that \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* shed. 
\v 32 The LORD will bring his bloodshed back upon his own head, for without the knowledge of \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* he struck down \zwd two men more righteous and better than he|\zwd* when he put to the sword \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|\zwd*|\zwd*, commander of Israel’s army, and \zwd Amasa son of \zwd Jether|\zwd*|\zwd*, commander of Judah’s army. 
\v 33 Their blood will come back upon the heads of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and his descendants forever; but for \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, his descendants, his house, and his throne, there shall be peace from the LORD forever.” 
\b
\m 
\v 34 So \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd* went up, struck down \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, and killed him. He was buried at his own home in the wilderness. 
\v 35 And \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* appointed \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd* in \zwd Joab|id="Q1141064" t="2"\zwd*’s place over the army, and he appointed \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd* in \zwd Abiathar|id="Q321804" t="2"\zwd*’s place. 
\s1 The Execution of Shimei
\b
\m 
\v 36 Then \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* summoned \zwd Shimei|\zwd* and said to him, “Build a house for yourself in Jerusalem and live there, but do not go anywhere else. 
\v 37 On the day you go out and cross the Kidron Valley, know for sure that you will die; your blood will be on your own head.” 
\b
\m 
\v 38 “The sentence is fair,” \zwd Shimei|\zwd* replied. “\zwd Your servant|\zwd* will do as \zwd my lord the king|\zwd* has spoken.” And \zwd Shimei|\zwd* lived in Jerusalem for a long time. 
\b
\m 
\v 39 After three years, however, two of \zwd Shimei|\zwd*’s slaves ran away to \zwd Achish son of \zwd Maacah|\zwd*|\zwd*,\f + \fr 2:39 \ft Maacah is a variant of Maoch; see 1 Samuel 27:2.\f* king of Gath. And \zwd Shimei|\zwd* was told, “Look, your slaves are in Gath.” 
\b
\m 
\v 40 So \zwd Shimei|\zwd* saddled his donkey and set out to \zwd Achish|\zwd* at Gath in search of his slaves, and he brought them back from Gath. 
\b
\m 
\v 41 When \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* was told that \zwd Shimei|\zwd* had gone from Jerusalem to Gath and had returned, 
\v 42 \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* summoned \zwd Shimei|\zwd* and said to him, “Did I not make you swear by the LORD and warn you, ‘On the day you leave and go elsewhere, know for sure that you will die’? And you told me, ‘The sentence is fair; I will comply.’ 
\v 43 So why have you not kept your oath to the LORD and the command that I gave you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 44 \zwd The king|Q37085\zwd* also said, “You know in your heart all the evil that you did to \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. Therefore the LORD will bring your evil back upon your head. 
\v 45 But \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* will be blessed and \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s throne will remain secure before the LORD forever.” 
\b
\m 
\v 46 Then \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* commanded \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*, and he went out and struck \zwd Shimei|\zwd* down, and he died. Thus the kingdom was firmly established in the hand of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*. 
\c 3
\s1 Solomon’s Prayer for Wisdom
\r (2 Chronicles 1:1–13; Psalms 45:1–17; Psalms 72:1–20)
\m 
\v 1 Later, \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* formed an alliance with \zwd Pharaoh king of Egypt|\zwd* by marrying \zwd his daughter|Q7180687\zwd*. \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* brought her to the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* until he had finished building his palace and the house of the LORD, as well as the wall around Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 The people, however, were still sacrificing on the high places because a house for the Name of the LORD had not yet been built. 
\v 3 And \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* loved the LORD and walked in the statutes of \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, except that he sacrificed and burned incense on the high places. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Now \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* went to Gibeon to sacrifice there, for it was the great high place. \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* offered a thousand burnt offerings on the altar there. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 One night at Gibeon the LORD appeared to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* in a dream, and God said, “Ask, and I will give it to you!” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* replied, “You have shown much loving devotion to \zwd Your servant|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, because he walked before You in faithfulness, righteousness, and uprightness of heart. And You have maintained this loving devotion by giving him a son to sit on his throne this very day. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 And now, O LORD my God, You have made \zwd Your servant|Q37085\zwd* king in \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s place. But I am only a little child, not knowing how to go out or come in. 
\v 8 \zwd Your servant|Q37085\zwd* is here among the people You have chosen, a people too numerous to count or number. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Therefore give \zwd Your servant|Q37085\zwd* an understanding heart to judge Your people and to discern between good and evil. For who is able to govern this great people of Yours?” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Now it pleased the Lord \f + \fr 3:10 \ft Hebrew Adonai; also in verse 15\f* that \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had made this request. 
\v 11 So God said to him, “Since you have asked for this instead of requesting long life or wealth for yourself or death for your enemies—but you have asked for discernment to administer justice—
\v 12 behold, I will do what you have asked. I will give you a wise and discerning heart, so that there will never have been another like you, nor will there ever be. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Moreover, I will give you what you did not request—both riches and honor—so that during all your days no man in any kingdom will be your equal. 
\v 14 So if you walk in My ways and keep My statutes and commandments, just as \zwd your father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* did, I will prolong your days.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* awoke, and indeed it had been a dream. So he returned to Jerusalem, stood before the ark of the covenant of the Lord, and offered burnt offerings and peace offerings. Then he held a feast for all his servants. 
\s1 Solomon Judges Wisely
\b
\m 
\v 16 At that time two prostitutes came to \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* and stood before him. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 One woman said, “Please, my lord, this woman and I live in the same house, and I gave birth while she was in the house. 
\v 18 On the third day after I gave birth, this woman also had a baby. We were alone, with no one in the house but the two of us. 
\v 19 During the night this woman’s son died because she rolled over on him. 
\v 20 So she got up in the middle of the night and took my son from my side while I was asleep. She laid him in her bosom and put her dead son at my bosom. 
\v 21 The next morning, when I got up to nurse my son, I discovered he was dead. But when I examined him, I realized that he was not the son I had borne.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 “No,” said the other woman, “the living one is my son and the dead one is your son.” 
\b
\m But the first woman insisted, “No, the dead one is yours and the living one is mine.” So they argued before \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Then \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* replied, “This woman says, ‘My son is alive and yours is dead,’ but that woman says, ‘No, your son is dead and mine is alive.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 \zwd The king|Q37085\zwd* continued, “Bring me a sword.” So they brought him a sword, 
\v 25 and \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* declared, “Cut the living child in two and give half to one and half to the other.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 Then the woman whose son was alive spoke to \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* because she yearned with compassion for her son. “Please, my lord,” she said, “give her the living baby. Do not kill him!” 
\b
\m But the other woman said, “He will be neither mine nor yours. Cut him in two!” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 Then \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* gave his ruling: “Give the living baby to the first woman. By no means should you kill him; she is his mother.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 When all Israel heard of the judgment \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* had given, they stood in awe of him, for they saw that the wisdom of God was in him to administer justice. 
\c 4
\s1 Solomon’s Princes
\m 
\v 1 So \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* ruled over Israel, 
\v 2 and these were his chief officials: 
\b
\li1 \zwd Azariah son of \zwd Zadok|id="Q140100" t="2"\zwd*|Q114905766\zwd* was the priest; 
\b
\li1 
\v 3 \zwd Elihoreph|Q133306277\zwd* and \zwd Ahijah|Q124554205\zwd*, the sons of \zwd Shisha|id="Q133446358" t="2"\zwd*,\f + \fr 4:3 \ft Shisha is also called Seraiah, Sheva, and Shavsha; see 2 Samuel 8:17, 2 Samuel 20:25, and 1 Chronicles 18:16.\f* were secretaries; 
\b
\li1 \zwd Jehoshaphat son of \zwd Ahilud|id="Q132156345" t="2"\zwd*|Q20502574\zwd* was the recorder; 
\b
\li1 
\v 4 \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd* was in charge of the army; 
\b
\li1 \zwd Zadok|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* were priests; 
\b
\li1 
\v 5 \zwd Azariah son of \zwd Nathan|\zwd*|Q139393705\zwd* was in charge of the governors; 
\b
\li1 \zwd Zabud son of \zwd Nathan|\zwd*|\zwd* was a priest and adviser to \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd*; 
\b
\li1 
\v 6 \zwd Ahishar|\zwd* was in charge of the palace; 
\b
\li1 and \zwd Adoniram \f + \fr 4:6 \ft Adoniram is a variant of Adoram and Hadoram; see 2 Samuel 20:24 and 2 Chronicles 10:18.\f* son of \zwd Abda|id="Q123121734" t="2"\zwd*|Q4057937\zwd* was in charge of the forced labor. 
\s1 Solomon’s Twelve Officers
\b
\m 
\v 7 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had \zwd twelve governors|\zwd* over all Israel to provide food for \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* and his household. Each one would arrange provisions for one month of the year, 
\v 8 and these were their names: 
\b
\li1 \zwd Ben-hur|\zwd* in the hill country of Ephraim; 
\b
\li1 
\v 9 \zwd Ben-deker|Q133300641\zwd* in Makaz, in Shaalbim, in Beth-shemesh, and in Elon-beth-hanan; 
\b
\li1 
\v 10 \zwd Ben-hesed|Q133305413\zwd* in Arubboth (Socoh and all the land of Hepher belonged to him); 
\b
\li1 
\v 11 \zwd Ben-abinadab|Q140235999\zwd* in Naphath-dor \f + \fr 4:11 \ft Or in all the heights of Dor; Naphath-dor is a variant of Naphoth-dor; see Joshua 11:2.\f* (\zwd Taphath|Q9355142\zwd*, a daughter of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, was his wife); 
\b
\li1 
\v 12 \zwd Baana son of \zwd Ahilud|\zwd*|\zwd* in Taanach, in Megiddo, and in all of Beth-shean next to Zarethan below Jezreel, from Beth-shean to Abel-meholah and on past Jokmeam; 
\b
\li1 
\v 13 \zwd Ben-geber|Q133311031\zwd* in Ramoth-gilead (the villages of \zwd Jair son of \zwd Manasseh|id="Q1056931" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q110159933" t="2"\zwd* in Gilead belonged to him, as well as the region of Argob in Bashan with its sixty great cities with walls and bronze bars); 
\b
\li1 
\v 14 \zwd Ahinadab son of \zwd Iddo|\zwd*|Q4695120\zwd* in Mahanaim; 
\b
\li1 
\v 15 \zwd Ahimaaz|Q2904547\zwd* in Naphtali (he had married \zwd Basemath|Q9166659\zwd*, a daughter of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*); 
\b
\li1 
\v 16 \zwd Baana son of \zwd Hushai|id="Q5949258" t="2"\zwd*|Q139587667\zwd* in Asher and in Aloth; 
\b
\li1 
\v 17 \zwd Jehoshaphat son of \zwd Paruah|\zwd*|\zwd* in Issachar; 
\b
\li1 
\v 18 \zwd Shimei son of \zwd Ela|\zwd*|Q139973134\zwd* in Benjamin; 
\b
\li1 
\v 19 \zwd Geber son of \zwd Uri|\zwd*|\zwd* in the land of Gilead, including the territories of \zwd Sihon king of the Amorites|id="Q887987" t="2"\zwd* and of \zwd Og king of Bashan|Q878675\zwd*. 
\b
\m There was also \zwd one governor|\zwd* in the land of Judah.\f + \fr 4:19 \ft Some LXX manuscripts; Hebrew And he was the one governor in the land.\f* 
\s1 Solomon’s Prosperity
\b
\m 
\v 20 The people of Judah and Israel were as numerous as the sand on the seashore, and they were eating and drinking and rejoicing. 
\v 21 And \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* reigned over all the kingdoms from the Euphrates \f + \fr 4:21 \ft Hebrew the River; also in verse 24\f* to the land of the Philistines, as far as the border of Egypt. These kingdoms offered tribute and served \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* all the days of his life. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s provisions for a single day were thirty cors of fine flour,\f + \fr 4:22 \ft 30 cors is approximately 187 bushels or 6,600 liters (probably about 5.5 tons or 5 metric tons of flour).\f* sixty cors of meal,\f + \fr 4:22 \ft 60 cors is approximately 375 bushels or 13,200 liters (probably about 11 tons or 10 metric tons of meal).\f* 
\v 23 ten fat oxen, twenty range oxen, and a hundred sheep, as well as deer, gazelles, roebucks, and fattened poultry. 
\v 24 For \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had dominion over everything west of the Euphrates \f + \fr 4:24 \ft Or beyond the River\f*—over all the kingdoms from Tiphsah to Gaza—and he had peace on all sides. 
\v 25 Throughout the days of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, Judah and Israel dwelt securely from Dan to Beersheba, each man under his own vine and his own fig tree. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had 4,000 \f + \fr 4:26 \ft Some LXX manuscripts (see also 2 Chronicles 9:25); Hebrew 40,000 \f* stalls for his chariot horses and 12,000 horses.\f + \fr 4:26 \ft Or horsemen or charioteers\f* 
\v 27 Each month the governors in turn provided food for \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* and all who came to his table. They saw to it that nothing was lacking. 
\v 28 Each one also brought to the required place their quotas of barley and straw for the chariot horses and other horses. 
\s1 Solomon’s Wisdom
\b
\m 
\v 29 And God gave \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* wisdom, exceedingly deep insight, and understanding beyond measure, like the sand on the seashore. 
\v 30 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s wisdom was greater than that of all the men of the East, greater than all the wisdom of Egypt. 
\v 31 He was wiser than all men—wiser than \zwd Ethan the Ezrahite|id="Q1370913" t="2"\zwd*, and wiser than \zwd Heman|\zwd*, \zwd Calcol|\zwd*, and \zwd Darda|\zwd*, the sons of \zwd Mahol|id="Q2415069" t="2"\zwd*. And his fame spread throughout the surrounding nations. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* composed three thousand proverbs, and his songs numbered a thousand and five. 
\v 33 He spoke of trees, from the cedar in Lebanon to the hyssop growing in the wall, and he taught about animals, birds, reptiles, and fish. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 So men of all nations came to listen to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s wisdom, sent by all the kings of the earth, who had heard of his wisdom. 
\c 5
\s1 Preparations for the Temple
\r (2 Chronicles 2:1–10)
\m 
\v 1 Now when \zwd Hiram king of Tyre|Q467115\zwd* heard that \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had been anointed king in \zwd his father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s place, he sent envoys to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*; for \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* had always been a friend of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 And \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* relayed this message to \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo 
\v 3 “As you are well aware, due to the wars waged on all sides against \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, he could not build a house for the Name of the LORD his God until the LORD had put his enemies under his feet. 
\v 4 But now the LORD my God has given me rest on every side, and there is no adversary or crisis. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 5 So behold, I plan to build a house for the Name of the LORD my God, according to what the LORD said to \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*: ‘I will put your son on your throne in your place, and he will build the house for My Name.’ 
\b
\pmo 
\v 6 Now therefore, order that cedars of Lebanon be cut down for me. My servants will be with your servants, and I will pay your servants whatever wages you set, for you know that there are none among us as skilled in logging as the Sidonians.” 
\s1 Hiram’s Reply to Solomon
\r (2 Chronicles 2:11–18)
\b
\m 
\v 7 When \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* received \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s message, he rejoiced greatly and said, “Blessed be the LORD this day! He has given \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* a wise son over this great people!” 
\v 8 Then \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* sent a reply to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, saying: 
\b
\pmo “I have received your message; I will do all you desire regarding the cedar and cypress \f + \fr 5:8 \ft Or pine or juniper or fir; also in verse 10\f* timber. 
\v 9 My servants will haul the logs from Lebanon to the Sea,\f + \fr 5:9 \ft That is, the Mediterranean Sea, also called the Great Sea\f* and I will float them as rafts by sea to the place you specify. There I will separate the logs, and you can take them away. And in exchange, you can meet my needs by providing my household with food.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* provided \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* with all the cedar and cypress timber he wanted, 
\v 11 and year after year \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* would provide \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* with 20,000 cors of wheat \f + \fr 5:11 \ft 20,000 cors is approximately 124,800 bushels or 4.4 million liters (probably about 3,800 tons or 3,400 metric tons of wheat).\f* as food for his household, as well as 20,000 baths of pure olive oil.\f + \fr 5:11 \ft LXX (see also 2 Chronicles 2:10); 20,000 baths is approximately 116,000 gallons or 440,000 liters of olive oil; Hebrew twenty cors of pure oil or twenty cors of pressed oil\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 12 And the LORD gave \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* wisdom, as He had promised him. There was peace between \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* and \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, and the two of them made a treaty.\f + \fr 5:12 \ft Forms of the Hebrew berit are translated in most passages as covenant.\f* 
\s1 Solomon’s Labor Force
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* conscripted a labor force of 30,000 men from all Israel. 
\v 14 He sent them to Lebanon in monthly shifts of 10,000 men, so that they would spend one month in Lebanon and two months at home. And \zwd Adoniram|\zwd* was in charge of the forced labor. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had 70,000 porters and 80,000 stonecutters in the mountains, 
\v 16 not including his 3,300 \f + \fr 5:16 \ft Hebrew; some LXX manuscripts 3,600; see 2 Chronicles 2:18.\f* foremen who supervised the workers. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 And \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* commanded them to quarry large, costly stones to lay the foundation of the temple with dressed stones. 
\v 18 So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s and \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd*’s builders, along with the Gebalites, quarried the stone and prepared the timber and stone for the construction of the temple. 
\c 6
\s1 Temple Construction Begins
\r (2 Chronicles 3:1–2)
\m 
\v 1 In the four hundred and eightieth \f + \fr 6:1 \ft Hebrew; LXX four hundred and fortieth\f* year after the Israelites had come out of the land of Egypt, in the fourth year of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s reign over Israel, in the month of Ziv,\f + \fr 6:1 \ft Ziv was the second month of the ancient Hebrew lunar calendar, usually occurring within the months of April and May; also in verse 37.\f* the second month, he began to build the house of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 The house that \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* built for the LORD was sixty cubits long, twenty cubits wide, and thirty cubits high.\f + \fr 6:2 \ft The house was approximately 90 feet long, 30 feet wide, and 45 feet high (27.4 meters long, 9.1 meters wide, and 13.7 meters high).\f* 
\v 3 The portico at the front of the main hall of the temple was twenty cubits \f + \fr 6:3 \ft 20 cubits is approximately 30 feet or 9.1 meters; also in verses 16 and 20.\f* long, extending across the width of the temple and projecting out ten cubits \f + \fr 6:3 \ft 10 cubits is approximately 15 feet or 4.6 meters; also in verses 23–26.\f* in front of the temple. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 He also had narrow windows framed high in the temple. 
\s1 The Chambers
\b
\m 
\v 5 Against the walls of the temple and the inner sanctuary, \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* built a chambered structure around the temple, in which he constructed the side rooms. 
\v 6 The bottom floor was five cubits wide,\f + \fr 6:6 \ft 5 cubits is approximately 7.5 feet or 2.3 meters; also in verses 10 and 24.\f* the middle floor six cubits,\f + \fr 6:6 \ft 6 cubits is approximately 9 feet or 2.7 meters.\f* and the third floor seven cubits.\f + \fr 6:6 \ft 7 cubits is approximately 10.5 feet or 3.2 meters.\f* He also placed offset ledges around the outside of the temple, so that nothing would be inserted into its walls. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 The temple was constructed using finished stones cut at the quarry, so that no hammer or chisel or any other iron tool was heard in the temple while it was being built. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 The entrance to the bottom \f + \fr 6:8 \ft LXX and Targum; Hebrew middle\f* floor was on the south side of the temple. A stairway led up to the middle level, and from there to the third floor. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* built the temple and finished it, roofing it with beams and planks of cedar. 
\v 10 He built chambers all along the temple, each five cubits high and attached to the temple with beams of cedar. 
\s1 God’s Promise to Solomon
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then the word of the LORD came to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, saying: 
\v 12 “As for this temple you are building, if you walk in My statutes, carry out My ordinances, and keep all My commandments by walking in them, I will fulfill through you the promise I made to \zwd your father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 13 And I will dwell among the Israelites and will not abandon My people Israel.” 
\s1 The Temple’s Interior
\r (2 Chronicles 3:5–9)
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* built the temple and finished it. 
\v 15 He lined the interior walls with cedar paneling from the floor of the temple to the ceiling, and he covered the floor with cypress \f + \fr 6:15 \ft Or pine or juniper or fir; also in verse 34\f* boards. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 He partitioned off the twenty cubits at the rear of the temple with cedar boards from floor to ceiling to form within the temple an inner sanctuary, the Most Holy Place.\f + \fr 6:16 \ft Or the Holy of Holies\f* 
\v 17 And the main hall in front of this room was forty cubits long.\f + \fr 6:17 \ft 40 cubits is approximately 60 feet or 18.3 meters.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 18 The cedar paneling inside the temple was carved with gourds and open flowers. Everything was cedar; not a stone could be seen. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* also prepared the inner sanctuary within the temple to set the ark of the covenant of the LORD there. 
\v 20 The inner sanctuary was twenty cubits long, twenty cubits wide, and twenty cubits high. He overlaid the inside with pure gold, and he also overlaid the altar of cedar.\f + \fr 6:20 \ft Or with cedar\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* overlaid the inside of the temple with pure gold, and he extended gold chains \f + \fr 6:21 \ft Or made gold chains to draw (the curtains)\f* across the front of the inner sanctuary, which was overlaid with gold. 
\v 22 So he overlaid with gold the whole interior of the temple, until everything was completely finished. He also overlaid with gold the entire altar that belonged to the inner sanctuary. 
\s1 The Cherubim
\r (2 Chronicles 3:10–13)
\b
\m 
\v 23 In the inner sanctuary he made two cherubim, each ten cubits high, out of olive wood. 
\v 24 One wing of the first cherub was five cubits long, and the other wing was five cubits long as well. So the full wingspan was ten cubits. 
\v 25 The second cherub also measured ten cubits; both cherubim had the same size and shape, 
\v 26 and the height of each cherub was ten cubits. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 And he placed the cherubim inside the innermost room of the temple. Since their wings were spread out, the wing of the first cherub touched one wall, while the wing of the second cherub touched the other wall, and in the middle of the room their wingtips touched. 
\v 28 He also overlaid the cherubim with gold. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 Then he carved the walls all around the temple, in both the inner and outer sanctuaries, with carved engravings of cherubim, palm trees, and open flowers. 
\v 30 And he overlaid the temple floor with gold in both the inner and outer sanctuaries. 
\s1 The Doors
\b
\m 
\v 31 For the entrance to the inner sanctuary, \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* constructed doors of olive wood with five-sided doorposts. 
\v 32 The double doors were made of olive wood, and he carved into them cherubim, palm trees, and open flowers and overlaid the cherubim and palm trees with hammered gold. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 In the same way he made four-sided doorposts of olive wood for the sanctuary entrance. 
\v 34 The two doors were made of cypress wood, and each had two folding panels. 
\v 35 He carved into them cherubim, palm trees, and open flowers, and he overlaid them with gold hammered evenly over the carvings. 
\s1 The Courtyard
\b
\m 
\v 36 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* built the inner courtyard with three rows of dressed stone and one row of trimmed cedar beams. 
\b
\m 
\v 37 The foundation of the house of the LORD was laid in the fourth year of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s reign, in the month of Ziv. 
\v 38 In the eleventh year, in the month of Bul,\f + \fr 6:38 \ft Bul was the eighth month of the ancient Hebrew lunar calendar, usually occurring within the months of October and November.\f* the eighth month, the temple was finished in every detail and according to every specification. So he built the temple in seven years. 
\c 7
\s1 Solomon’s Palace Complex
\m 
\v 1 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, however, took thirteen years to complete the construction of his entire palace. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 He built the House of the Forest of Lebanon a hundred cubits long, fifty cubits wide, and thirty cubits high,\f + \fr 7:2 \ft The house was approximately 150 feet long, 75 feet wide, and 45 feet high (45.7 meters long, 22.9 meters wide, and 13.7 meters high).\f* with four rows of cedar pillars supporting the cedar beams. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 The house was roofed with cedar above the beams that rested on the pillars—forty-five beams, fifteen per row. 
\v 4 There were three rows of high windows facing one another in three tiers. 
\v 5 All the doorways \f + \fr 7:5 \ft Literally doorways and doorposts\f* had rectangular frames, with the openings facing one another in three tiers. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* made his colonnade fifty cubits long and thirty cubits wide,\f + \fr 7:6 \ft The colonnade was approximately 75 feet long and 45 feet wide (22.9 meters long and 13.7 meters wide).\f* with a portico in front of it and a canopy with pillars in front of the portico. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 In addition, he built a hall for the throne, the Hall of Justice, where he was to judge. It was paneled with cedar from floor to ceiling.\f + \fr 7:7 \ft Syriac and Vulgate; Hebrew from floor to floor\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And the palace where \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* would live, set further back, was of similar construction. He also made a palace like this hall for \zwd \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s daughter|Q7180687\zwd*, whom he had married. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 All these buildings were constructed with costly stones, cut to size and trimmed with saws inside and out from the foundation to the eaves, and from the outside to the great courtyard. 
\v 10 The foundations were laid with large, costly stones, some ten cubits long \f + \fr 7:10 \ft 10 cubits is approximately 15 feet or 4.6 meters.\f* and some eight cubits long.\f + \fr 7:10 \ft 8 cubits is approximately 12 feet or 3.7 meters.\f* 
\v 11 Above these were costly stones, cut to size, and cedar beams. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 The great courtyard was surrounded by three rows of dressed stone and a row of trimmed cedar beams, as were the inner courtyard and portico of the house of the LORD. 
\s1 The Pillars and Capitals
\r (2 Chronicles 3:14–17)
\b
\m 
\v 13 Now \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* sent to bring \zwd Huram|Q108422651\zwd* \f + \fr 7:13 \ft Hebrew Hiram, a variant of Huram; also in verses 40 and 45; see 2 Chronicles 4:11. Note that this is not Hiram king of Tyre mentioned in 1 Kings 5:1.\f* from Tyre. 
\v 14 He was the son of \zwd a widow|\zwd* from the tribe of Naphtali, and \zwd his father|\zwd* was a man of Tyre, a craftsman in bronze. \zwd Huram|Q108422651\zwd* had great skill, understanding, and knowledge for every kind of bronze work. So he came to \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* and carried out all his work. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 He cast two pillars of bronze, each eighteen cubits high and twelve cubits in circumference.\f + \fr 7:15 \ft Each pillar was approximately 27 feet high and 18 feet in circumference (8.2 meters high and 5.5 meters in circumference).\f* 
\v 16 He also made two capitals of cast bronze to set on top of the pillars, each capital five cubits high.\f + \fr 7:16 \ft 5 cubits is approximately 7.5 feet or 2.3 meters.\f* 
\v 17 For the capitals on top of the pillars he made a network of lattice, with wreaths of chainwork, seven for each capital.\f + \fr 7:17 \ft Hebrew; LXX one for each capital\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Likewise, he made the pillars with two rows of pomegranates around each grating to cover each capital atop the pillars. 
\v 19 And the capitals atop the pillars in the portico were shaped like lilies, four cubits high.\f + \fr 7:19 \ft 4 cubits is approximately 6 feet or 1.8 meters; also in verse 38.\f* 
\v 20 On the capitals of both pillars, just above the rounded projection next to the network, were the two hundred pomegranates in rows encircling each capital. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Thus he set up the pillars at the portico of the temple. The pillar to the south he named Jachin,\f + \fr 7:21 \ft Jachin probably means He establishes.\f* and the pillar to the north he named Boaz.\f + \fr 7:21 \ft Boaz probably means in Him is strength.\f* 
\v 22 And the tops of the pillars were shaped like lilies. So the work of the pillars was completed. 
\s1 The Molten Sea
\r (2 Chronicles 4:1–5)
\b
\m 
\v 23 He also made the Sea of cast metal. It was circular in shape, measuring ten cubits from rim to rim, five cubits in height, and thirty cubits in circumference.\f + \fr 7:23 \ft The Sea was approximately 15 feet from rim to rim, 7.5 feet in height, and 45 feet in circumference (4.6 meters from rim to rim, 2.3 meters in height, and 13.7 meters in circumference).\f* 
\v 24 Below the rim, ornamental buds encircled it, ten per cubit all the way around the Sea, cast in two rows as a part of the Sea. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 The Sea stood on twelve oxen, three facing north, three facing west, three facing south, and three facing east. The Sea rested on them, with all their hindquarters toward the center. 
\v 26 It was a handbreadth thick,\f + \fr 7:26 \ft A handbreadth is approximately 2.9 inches or 7.4 centimeters.\f* and its rim was fashioned like the brim of a cup, like a lily blossom. It could hold two thousand baths.\f + \fr 7:26 \ft 2,000 baths is approximately 11,600 gallons or 44,000 liters; LXX does not include this sentence.\f* 
\s1 The Ten Bronze Stands
\b
\m 
\v 27 In addition, he made ten movable stands of bronze, each four cubits long, four cubits wide, and three cubits high.\f + \fr 7:27 \ft The stands were approximately 6 feet in length and width, and 4.5 feet high (1.8 meters in length and width, and 1.4 meters high).\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 28 This was the design of the stands: They had side panels attached to uprights, 
\v 29 and on the panels between the uprights were lions, oxen, and cherubim. On the uprights was a pedestal above, and below the lions and oxen were wreaths of beveled work. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Each stand had four bronze wheels with bronze axles and a basin resting on four supports, with wreaths at each side. 
\v 31 The opening to each stand inside the crown at the top was one cubit deep,\f + \fr 7:31 \ft One cubit is approximately 18 inches or 45.7 centimeters.\f* with a round opening like the design of a pedestal, a cubit and a half wide.\f + \fr 7:31 \ft A cubit and a half is approximately 2.25 feet or 68.6 centimeters wide; similarly in verse 32.\f* And around its opening were engravings, but the panels of the stands were square, not round. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 There were four wheels under the panels, and the axles of the wheels were attached to the stand; each wheel was a cubit and a half in diameter. 
\v 33 The wheels were made like chariot wheels; their axles, rims, spokes, and hubs were all of cast metal. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 Each stand had four handles, one for each corner, projecting from the stand. 
\v 35 At the top of each stand was a circular band half a cubit high.\f + \fr 7:35 \ft Half a cubit is approximately 9 inches or 22.9 centimeters high.\f* The supports and panels were cast as a unit with the top of the stand. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 He engraved cherubim, lions, and palm trees on the surfaces of the supports and panels, wherever each had space, with wreaths all around. 
\v 37 In this way he made the ten stands, each with the same casting, dimensions, and shape. 
\s1 The Ten Bronze Basins
\r (2 Chronicles 4:6–8)
\b
\m 
\v 38 He also made ten bronze basins, each holding forty baths \f + \fr 7:38 \ft 40 baths is approximately 232 gallons or 880 liters.\f* and measuring four cubits across, one basin for each of the ten stands. 
\b
\m 
\v 39 He set five stands on the south side of the temple and five on the north, and he put the Sea on the south side, at the southeast corner of the temple. 
\s1 Completion of the Bronze Works
\r (2 Chronicles 4:11–18)
\b
\m 
\v 40 Additionally, \zwd Huram|Q108422651\zwd* made the pots,\f + \fr 7:40 \ft Many Hebrew manuscripts, LXX, Syriac, and Vulgate (see also verse 45 and 2 Chronicles 4:11); many other Hebrew manuscripts basins\f* shovels, and sprinkling bowls. 
\b
\m So \zwd Huram|Q108422651\zwd* finished all the work that he had undertaken for \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* in the house of the LORD: 
\b
\li1 
\v 41 the two pillars; 
\b
\li1 the two bowl-shaped capitals atop the pillars; 
\b
\li1 the two sets of network covering both bowls of the capitals atop the pillars; 
\b
\li1 
\v 42 the four hundred pomegranates for the two sets of network (two rows of pomegranates for each network covering both the bowl-shaped capitals atop the pillars); 
\b
\li1 
\v 43 the ten stands; 
\b
\li1 the ten basins on the stands; 
\b
\li1 
\v 44 the Sea; 
\b
\li1 the twelve oxen underneath the Sea; 
\b
\li1 
\v 45 and the pots, shovels, and sprinkling bowls. 
\b
\m All the articles that \zwd Huram|Q108422651\zwd* made for \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* in the house of the LORD were made of burnished bronze. 
\v 46 \zwd The king|Q37085\zwd* had them cast in clay molds in the plain of the Jordan between Succoth and Zarethan.\f + \fr 7:46 \ft Zarethan is a variant of Zeredah; see 2 Chronicles 4:17.\f* 
\v 47 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* left all these articles unweighed, because there were so many. The weight of the bronze could not be determined. 
\s1 Completion of the Gold Furnishings
\r (2 Chronicles 4:19–22)
\b
\m 
\v 48 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* also made all the furnishings for the house of the LORD: 
\b
\li1 the golden altar; 
\b
\li1 the golden table on which was placed the Bread of the Presence; 
\b
\li1 
\v 49 the lampstands of pure gold in front of the inner sanctuary, five on the right side and five on the left; 
\b
\li1 the gold flowers, lamps, and tongs; 
\b
\li1 
\v 50 the pure gold basins, wick trimmers, sprinkling bowls, ladles, and censers; 
\b
\li1 and the gold hinges for the doors of the inner temple (that is, the Most Holy Place \f + \fr 7:50 \ft Or the Holy of Holies\f*) as well as for the doors of the main hall of the temple. 
\b
\m 
\v 51 So all the work that \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had performed for the house of the LORD was completed. 
\b
\m Then \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* brought in the items \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* had dedicated—the silver, the gold, and the furnishings—and he placed them in the treasuries of the house of the LORD. 
\c 8
\s1 The Ark Enters the Temple
\r (2 Chronicles 5:1–14)
\m 
\v 1 At that time \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* assembled before him in Jerusalem the elders of Israel—all the tribal heads and family leaders of the Israelites—to bring up the ark of the covenant of the LORD from Zion, the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 2 And all the men of Israel came together to \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* at the feast in the seventh month,\f + \fr 8:2 \ft That is, the Feast of Tabernacles (or Booths or Shelters); similarly in verse 65; see Leviticus 23:33–36.\f* the month of Ethanim.\f + \fr 8:2 \ft Ethanim was the seventh month of the ancient Hebrew lunar calendar, usually occurring within the months of September and October.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 3 When all the elders of Israel had arrived, the priests took up the ark, 
\v 4 and they brought up the ark of the LORD and the Tent of Meeting with all its sacred furnishings. So the priests and Levites carried them up. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 There, before the ark, \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* and the whole congregation of Israel who had assembled with him sacrificed so many sheep and oxen that they could not be counted or numbered. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then the priests brought the ark of the covenant of the LORD to its place in the inner sanctuary of the temple, the Most Holy Place,\f + \fr 8:6 \ft Or the Holy of Holies\f* beneath the wings of the cherubim. 
\v 7 For the cherubim spread their wings over the place of the ark and overshadowed the ark and its poles. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 The poles extended far enough that their ends were visible from the Holy Place in front of the inner sanctuary, but not from outside the Holy Place;\f + \fr 8:8 \ft Literally not from outside\f* and they are there to this day. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 There was nothing in the ark except the two stone tablets that \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd* had placed in it at Horeb,\f + \fr 8:9 \ft That is, Mount Sinai, or possibly a mountain in the range containing Mount Sinai\f* where the LORD had made a covenant with the Israelites after they had come out of the land of Egypt. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 And when the priests came out of the Holy Place, the cloud filled the house of the LORD 
\v 11 so that the priests could not stand there to minister because of the cloud. For the glory of the LORD filled the house of the LORD. 
\s1 Solomon Blesses the LORD
\r (2 Chronicles 6:1–11)
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* declared: 
\b
\q1 “The LORD \f + \fr 8:12 \ft Some LXX manuscripts The Lord has set the sun in the heavens, but\f* has said that He would dwell 
\q2 in the thick cloud. 
\q1 
\v 13 I have indeed built You an exalted house, 
\q2 a place for You to dwell forever.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 And as the whole assembly of Israel stood there, the king turned around and blessed them all 
\v 15 and said: 
\b
\pmo “Blessed be the LORD, the God of Israel, who has fulfilled with His own hand what He spoke with His mouth to \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, saying, 
\v 16 ‘Since the day I brought My people Israel out of Egypt, I have not chosen a city from any tribe of Israel in which to build a house so that My Name would be there. But I have chosen \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* to be over My people Israel.’ 
\b
\pmo 
\v 17 Now it was in the heart of \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* to build a house for the Name of the LORD, the God of Israel. 
\v 18 But the LORD said to \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, ‘Since it was in your heart to build a house for My Name, you have done well to have this in your heart. 
\v 19 Nevertheless, you are not the one to build it; but your son, your own offspring, will build the house for My Name.’ 
\b
\pmo 
\v 20 Now the LORD has fulfilled the word that He spoke. I have succeeded \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, and I sit on the throne of Israel, as the LORD promised. I have built the house for the Name of the LORD, the God of Israel. 
\v 21 And there I have provided a place for the ark, which contains the covenant of the LORD that He made with our fathers when He brought them out of the land of Egypt.” 
\s1 Solomon’s Prayer of Dedication
\r (2 Chronicles 6:12–42)
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* stood before the altar of the LORD in front of the whole assembly of Israel, spread out his hands toward heaven, 
\v 23 and said: 
\b
\pmo “O LORD, God of Israel, there is no God like You in heaven above or on earth below, keeping Your covenant of loving devotion with Your servants who walk before You with all their hearts. 
\v 24 You have kept Your promise to Your servant, \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. What You spoke with Your mouth You have fulfilled with Your hand this day. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 25 Therefore now, O LORD, God of Israel, keep for Your servant, \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, what You promised when You said: ‘You will never fail to have a man to sit before Me on the throne of Israel, if only your descendants guard their way to walk before Me as you have done.’ 
\v 26 And now, O God of Israel, please confirm what You promised to Your servant, \zwd my father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 27 But will God indeed dwell upon the earth? The heavens, even the highest heavens, cannot contain You, much less this temple I have built. 
\v 28 Yet regard the prayer and plea of Your servant, O LORD my God, so that You may hear the cry and the prayer that Your servant is praying before You today. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 29 May Your eyes be open toward this temple night and day, toward the place of which You said, ‘My Name shall be there,’ so that You may hear the prayer that Your servant prays toward this place. 
\v 30 Hear the plea of Your servant and of Your people Israel when they pray toward this place. May You hear from heaven, Your dwelling place. May You hear and forgive. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 31 When a man sins against his neighbor and is required to take an oath, and he comes to take an oath before Your altar in this temple, 
\v 32 then may You hear from heaven and act. May You judge Your servants, condemning the wicked man by bringing down on his own head what he has done, and justifying the righteous man by rewarding him according to his righteousness. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 33 When Your people Israel are defeated before an enemy because they have sinned against You, and they return to You and confess Your name, praying and pleading with You in this temple, 
\v 34 then may You hear from heaven and forgive the sin of Your people Israel. May You restore them to the land You gave to their fathers. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 35 When the skies are shut and there is no rain because Your people have sinned against You, and they pray toward this place and confess Your name, and they turn from their sins because You have afflicted them, 
\v 36 then may You hear from heaven and forgive the sin of Your servants, Your people Israel, so that You may teach them the good way in which they should walk. May You send rain on the land that You gave Your people as an inheritance. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 37 When famine or plague comes upon the land, or blight or mildew or locusts or grasshoppers, or when their enemy besieges them in their cities, whatever plague or sickness may come, 
\v 38 then may whatever prayer or petition Your people Israel make—each knowing his own afflictions and spreading out his hands toward this temple—
\v 39 be heard by You from heaven, Your dwelling place. And may You forgive and act, and repay each man according to all his ways, since You know his heart—for You alone know the hearts of all men—
\v 40 so that they may fear You all the days they live in the land that You gave to our fathers. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 41 And as for the foreigner who is not of Your people Israel but has come from a distant land because of Your name—
\v 42 for they will hear of Your great name and mighty hand and outstretched arm—when he comes and prays toward this temple, 
\v 43 then may You hear from heaven, Your dwelling place, and do according to all for which the foreigner calls to You. Then all the peoples of the earth will know Your name and fear You, as do Your people Israel, and they will know that this house I have built is called by Your Name. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 44 When Your people go to war against their enemies, wherever You send them, and when they pray to the LORD in the direction of the city You have chosen and the house I have built for Your Name, 
\v 45 then may You hear from heaven their prayer and their plea, and may You uphold their cause. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 46 When they sin against You—for there is no one who does not sin—and You become angry with them and deliver them to an enemy who takes them as captives to his own land, whether far or near, 
\v 47 and when they come to their senses in the land to which they were taken, and they repent and plead with You in the land of their captors, saying, ‘We have sinned and done wrong; we have acted wickedly,’ 
\v 48 and when they return to You with all their heart and soul in the land of the enemies who took them captive, and when they pray to You in the direction of the land that You gave to their fathers, the city You have chosen, and the house I have built for Your Name, 
\v 49 then may You hear from heaven, Your dwelling place, their prayer and petition, and may You uphold their cause. 
\v 50 May You forgive Your people who have sinned against You and all the transgressions they have committed against You, and may You grant them compassion in the eyes of their captors to show them mercy. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 51 For they are Your people and Your inheritance; You brought them out of Egypt, out of the furnace for iron. 
\v 52 May Your eyes be open to the pleas of Your servant and of Your people Israel, and may You listen to them whenever they call to You. 
\v 53 For You, O Lord GOD, have set them apart from all the peoples of the earth as Your inheritance, as You spoke through Your servant Moses when You brought our fathers out of Egypt.” 
\s1 Solomon’s Benediction
\b
\m 
\v 54 Now when \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had finished praying this entire prayer and petition to the LORD, he got up before the altar of the LORD, where he had been kneeling with his hands spread out toward heaven. 
\v 55 And he stood and blessed the whole assembly of Israel in a loud voice, saying: 
\b
\pmo 
\v 56 “Blessed be the LORD, who has given rest to His people Israel according to all that He promised. Not one word has failed of all the good promises He made through \zwd His servant Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 57 May the LORD our God be with us, as He was with our fathers. May He never leave us or forsake us. 
\v 58 May He incline our hearts to Himself, to walk in all His ways and to keep the commandments and statutes and ordinances He commanded our fathers. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 59 And may these words with which I have made my petition before the LORD be near to the LORD our God day and night, so that He may uphold the cause of \zwd His servant|Q37085\zwd* and of His people Israel as each day requires, 
\v 60 so that all the peoples of the earth may know that the LORD is God. There is no other! 
\b
\pmo 
\v 61 So let your heart be fully devoted to the LORD our God, as it is this day, to walk in His statutes and to keep His commandments.” 
\s1 Sacrifices of Dedication
\r (2 Chronicles 7:4–10)
\b
\m 
\v 62 Then \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* and all Israel with him offered sacrifices before the LORD. 
\v 63 And \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* offered as peace offerings to the LORD 22,000 oxen and 120,000 sheep. So \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* and all the Israelites dedicated the house of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 64 On that same day \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* consecrated the middle of the courtyard in front of the house of the LORD, and there he offered the burnt offerings, the grain offerings, and the fat of the peace offerings, since the bronze altar before the LORD was too small to contain all these offerings. 
\b
\m 
\v 65 So at that time \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* and all Israel with him—a great assembly of people from Lebo-hamath to the Brook of Egypt—kept the feast before the LORD our God for seven days and seven more days—fourteen days in all. 
\b
\m 
\v 66 On the fifteenth day \f + \fr 8:66 \ft Hebrew On the eighth day, probably referring to the day following the seven-day feast; see 2 Chronicles 7:9–10.\f* \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* sent the people away. So they blessed \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* and went home, joyful and glad in heart for all the good things that the LORD had done for \zwd His servant David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* and for His people Israel. 
\c 9
\s1 The LORD’s Response to Solomon
\r (2 Chronicles 7:11–22)
\m 
\v 1 Now when \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had finished building the house of the LORD and the royal palace, and had achieved all that he had desired to do, 
\v 2 the LORD appeared to him a second time, as He had appeared to him at Gibeon. 
\v 3 And the LORD said to him: 
\b
\pmo “I have heard your prayer and petition before Me. I have consecrated this temple, which you have built, by putting My Name there forever; My eyes and My heart will be there for all time. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 4 And as for you, if you walk before Me as \zwd your father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* walked, with a heart of integrity and uprightness, doing all I have commanded you, and if you keep My statutes and ordinances, 
\v 5 then I will establish your royal throne over Israel forever, as I promised \zwd your father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* when I said, ‘You will never fail to have a man on the throne of Israel.’ 
\b
\pmo 
\v 6 But if indeed you or your sons turn away from following Me and do not keep the commandments and statutes I have set before you, and if you go off to serve and worship other gods, 
\v 7 then I will cut off Israel from the land that I have given them, and I will banish from My presence this temple I have sanctified for My Name. Then Israel will become an object of scorn and ridicule among all peoples. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 8 And when this temple has become a heap of rubble,\f + \fr 9:8 \ft Some LXX manuscripts, Syriac, and Arabic; Hebrew And though this temple is now exalted; see also 2 Chronicles 7:21.\f* all who pass by it will be appalled and will hiss and say, ‘Why has the LORD done such a thing to this land and to this temple?’ 
\v 9 And others will answer, ‘Because they have forsaken the LORD their God who brought their fathers out of the land of Egypt, and have embraced other gods, worshiping and serving them—because of this, the LORD has brought all this disaster upon them.’” 
\s1 Solomon’s Additional Achievements
\r (2 Chronicles 8:1–18)
\b
\m 
\v 10 Now at the end of the twenty years during which \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* built these two houses, the house of the LORD and the royal palace, 
\v 11 \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* gave twenty towns in the land of Galilee to \zwd Hiram king of Tyre|Q467115\zwd*, who had supplied him with cedar and cypress \f + \fr 9:11 \ft Or pine or juniper or fir\f* logs and gold for his every desire. 
\v 12 So \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* went out from Tyre to inspect the towns that \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had given him, but he was not pleased with them. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 “What are these towns you have given me, \zwd my brother|Q37085\zwd*?” asked \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd*, and he called them the Land of Cabul,\f + \fr 9:13 \ft Cabul sounds like the Hebrew for good-for-nothing.\f* as they are called to this day. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 And \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* had sent \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* 120 talents of gold.\f + \fr 9:14 \ft 120 talents is approximately 4.52 tons or 4.1 metric tons of gold.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 15 This is the account of the forced labor that \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* imposed to build the house of the LORD, his own palace, the supporting terraces,\f + \fr 9:15 \ft Hebrew the Millo; also in verse 24\f* and the wall of Jerusalem, as well as Hazor, Megiddo, and Gezer. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 \zwd Pharaoh king of Egypt|\zwd* had attacked and captured Gezer. He had set it on fire, killed the Canaanites who lived in the city, and given it as a dowry to his daughter, \zwd \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s wife|Q7180687\zwd*. 
\v 17 So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* rebuilt Gezer, Lower Beth-horon, 
\v 18 Baalath, and Tamar \f + \fr 9:18 \ft Alternate MT reading; the other alternate reads Tadmor\f* in the Wilderness of Judah,\f + \fr 9:18 \ft Hebrew in the wilderness in the land\f* 
\v 19 as well as all the store cities that \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had for his chariots and horses \f + \fr 9:19 \ft Or horsemen or charioteers\f*—whatever he desired to build in Jerusalem, Lebanon, and throughout the land of his dominion. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 As for all the people who remained of the Amorites, Hittites, Perizzites, Hivites, and Jebusites (the people who were not Israelites)—
\v 21 their descendants who remained in the land, those whom the Israelites were unable to devote to destruction \f + \fr 9:21 \ft Forms of the Hebrew cherem refer to the giving over of things or persons to the LORD, either by destroying them or by giving them as an offering.\f*—\zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* conscripted these people to be forced laborers, as they are to this day. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 But \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* did not consign any of the Israelites to slavery, because they were his men of war, his servants, his officers, his captains, and the commanders of his chariots and cavalry. 
\v 23 They were also the chief officers over \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s projects: 550 supervisors over the people who did the work. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 As soon as \zwd \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s daughter|Q7180687\zwd* had come up from the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* to the palace that \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had built for her, he built the supporting terraces. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Three times a year \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* offered burnt offerings and peace offerings on the altar he had built for the LORD, burning incense with them before the LORD. So he completed the temple. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* also assembled a fleet of ships at Ezion-geber, which is near Eloth \f + \fr 9:26 \ft Eloth is a variant of Elath; see LXX, 2 Kings 14:22, and 2 Kings 16:6.\f* in Edom, on the shore of the Red Sea.\f + \fr 9:26 \ft Or the Sea of Reeds\f* 
\v 27 And \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* sent his servants, sailors who knew the sea, to serve in the fleet with \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s servants. 
\v 28 They sailed to Ophir and imported gold from there—420 talents \f + \fr 9:28 \ft 420 talents is approximately 15.8 tons or 14.4 metric tons of gold.\f*—and delivered it to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*. 
\c 10
\s1 The Queen of Sheba
\r (2 Chronicles 9:1–12)
\m 
\v 1 Now when \zwd the queen of Sheba|Q159888\zwd* heard about the fame of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* concerning the name of the LORD, she came to test him with difficult questions. 
\v 2 She arrived in Jerusalem with a very large caravan—with camels bearing spices, gold in great abundance, and precious stones. 
\b
\m And she came to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* and spoke to him all that was on her mind. 
\v 3 And \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* answered all her questions; nothing was too difficult for the king to explain. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 When \zwd the queen of Sheba|Q159888\zwd* saw all the wisdom of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, the palace he had built, 
\v 5 the food at his table, the seating of his servants, the service and attire of his attendants, his cupbearers, and the burnt offerings he presented at the house of the LORD, it took her breath away. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 She said to \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd*, “The report I heard in my own country about your words and wisdom is true. 
\v 7 But I did not believe these things until I came and saw with my own eyes. Indeed, not even half was told to me. Your wisdom and prosperity have far exceeded the report I heard. 
\v 8 How blessed are your men!\f + \fr 10:8 \ft LXX, Syriac, and Vulgate your wives\f* How blessed are these servants of yours who stand continually before you and hear your wisdom! 
\v 9 Blessed be the LORD your God, who has delighted in you to set you on the throne of Israel. Because of the LORD’s eternal love for Israel, He has made you king to carry out justice and righteousness.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then she gave \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* 120 talents of gold,\f + \fr 10:10 \ft 120 talents is approximately 4.52 tons or 4.1 metric tons of gold.\f* a great quantity of spices, and precious stones. Never again were spices in such abundance brought in as those \zwd the queen of Sheba|Q159888\zwd* gave to \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 (The fleet of \zwd Hiram|Q467115\zwd* that brought gold from Ophir also brought from Ophir a great cargo of almug \f + \fr 10:11 \ft Almug is probably a variant of algum; also in verse 12; see 2 Chronicles 2:8.\f* wood and precious stones. 
\v 12 \zwd The king|Q37085\zwd* made the almug wood into steps for the house of the LORD and for \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd*’s palace, and into lyres and harps for the singers. Never before had such almug wood been brought in, nor has such been seen again to this day.) 
\b
\m 
\v 13 \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* gave \zwd the queen of Sheba|Q159888\zwd* all she desired—whatever she asked—besides what he had given her out of his royal bounty. Then she left and returned to her own country, along with her servants. 
\s1 Solomon’s Wealth and Splendor
\r (2 Chronicles 1:14–17; 2 Chronicles 9:13–28)
\b
\m 
\v 14 The weight of gold that came to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* each year was 666 talents,\f + \fr 10:14 \ft 666 talents is approximately 25.1 tons or 22.8 metric tons of gold.\f* 
\v 15 not including the revenue from the merchants, traders, and all the Arabian kings and governors of the land. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* made two hundred large shields of hammered gold; six hundred shekels of gold \f + \fr 10:16 \ft 600 shekels is approximately 15.1 pounds or 6.8 kilograms of gold.\f* went into each shield. 
\v 17 He also made three hundred small shields of hammered gold; three minas of gold \f + \fr 10:17 \ft 3 minas is approximately 3.77 pounds or 1.71 kilograms of gold; possibly a reference to double minas, that is, approximately 7.54 pounds or 3.42 kilograms.\f* went into each shield. And \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* put them in the House of the Forest of Lebanon. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Additionally, \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* made a great throne of ivory and overlaid it with pure gold. 
\v 19 The throne had six steps, and its back had a rounded top. There were armrests on both sides of the seat, with a lion standing beside each armrest. 
\v 20 Twelve lions stood on the six steps, one at either end of each step. Nothing like this had ever been made for any kingdom. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 All \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s drinking cups were gold, and all the utensils of the House of the Forest of Lebanon were pure gold. There was no silver, because it was accounted as nothing in the days of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*. 
\v 22 For \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd* had the ships of Tarshish \f + \fr 10:22 \ft Or a fleet of trading ships; twice in this verse\f* at sea with Hiram’s fleet, and once every three years the ships of Tarshish would arrive bearing gold, silver, ivory, apes, and peacocks.\f + \fr 10:22 \ft Or baboons\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd* surpassed all the kings of the earth in riches and wisdom. 
\v 24 The whole world sought an audience with \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* to hear the wisdom that God had put in his heart. 
\v 25 Year after year, each visitor would bring his tribute: articles of silver and gold, clothing, weapons, spices, horses, and mules. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* accumulated \f + \fr 10:26 \ft Literally Solomon accumulated chariots and horses; he had\f* 1,400 chariots and 12,000 horses,\f + \fr 10:26 \ft Or horsemen or charioteers\f* which he stationed in the chariot cities and also with him in Jerusalem. 
\v 27 \zwd The king|Q37085\zwd* made silver as common in Jerusalem as stones, and cedar as abundant as sycamore in the foothills.\f + \fr 10:27 \ft Hebrew Shephelah or lowlands; that is, the western foothills of Judea\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 28 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s horses were imported from Egypt and Kue;\f + \fr 10:28 \ft Probably an area in Cilicia, a province in the southeast of Asia Minor\f* the royal merchants purchased them from Kue. 
\v 29 A chariot could be imported from Egypt for six hundred shekels of silver,\f + \fr 10:29 \ft 600 shekels is approximately 15.1 pounds or 6.8 kilograms of silver.\f* and a horse for a hundred and fifty.\f + \fr 10:29 \ft 150 shekels is approximately 3.8 pounds or 1.7 kilograms of silver.\f* Likewise, they exported them to all the kings of the Hittites and to the kings of Aram. 
\c 11
\s1 Solomon’s Foreign Wives
\m 
\v 1 \zwd King Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, however, loved many foreign women along with \zwd the daughter of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*|Q7180687\zwd*—women of Moab, Ammon, Edom, and Sidon, as well as Hittite women. 
\v 2 These women were from the nations about which the LORD had told the Israelites, “You must not intermarry with them, for surely they will turn your hearts after their gods.” Yet \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* clung to these women in love. 
\v 3 He had seven hundred wives of royal birth and three hundred concubines—and his wives turned his heart away. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 For when \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* grew old, his wives turned his heart after other gods, and he was not wholeheartedly devoted to the LORD his God, as \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* had been. 
\v 5 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* followed Ashtoreth the goddess of the Sidonians and Milcom \f + \fr 11:5 \ft Milcom is a variant of Molech; also in verse 33; see verse 7 and Leviticus 18:21.\f* the abomination of the Ammonites. 
\v 6 So \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* did evil in the sight of the LORD; unlike \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, he did not follow the LORD completely. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 At that time on a hill east of Jerusalem, \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* built a high place for Chemosh the abomination of Moab and for Molech the abomination of the Ammonites. 
\v 8 He did the same for all his foreign wives, who burned incense and sacrificed to their gods. 
\s1 God’s Anger against Solomon
\b
\m 
\v 9 Now the LORD grew angry with \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, because his heart had turned away from the LORD, the God of Israel, who had appeared to him twice. 
\v 10 Although He had warned \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* explicitly not to follow other gods, \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* did not keep the LORD’s command. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then the LORD said to \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, “Because you have done this and have not kept My covenant and My statutes, which I have commanded you, I will tear the kingdom away from you and give it to your servant. 
\v 12 Nevertheless, for the sake of \zwd your father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, I will not do it during your lifetime; I will tear it out of the hand of \zwd your son|\zwd*. 
\v 13 Yet I will not tear the whole kingdom away from him. I will give one tribe to \zwd your son|\zwd* for the sake of \zwd My servant David|\zwd* and for the sake of Jerusalem, which I have chosen.” 
\s1 Hadad’s Return
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then the LORD raised up against \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* an adversary, \zwd Hadad the Edomite|Q5637555\zwd*, from the royal line of Edom. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Earlier, when \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* was in Edom, \zwd Joab the commander of the army|id="Q1141064" t="2"\zwd* had gone to bury the dead and had struck down every male in Edom. 
\v 16 \zwd Joab|id="Q1141064" t="2"\zwd* and all Israel had stayed there six months, until he had killed every male in Edom. 
\v 17 But \zwd Hadad|Q5637555\zwd*, still just a young boy, had fled to Egypt, along with some Edomites who were servants of \zwd his father|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 \zwd Hadad|Q5637555\zwd* and his men set out from Midian and went to Paran. They took men from Paran with them and went to Egypt, to \zwd Pharaoh king of Egypt|\zwd*, who gave \zwd Hadad|Q5637555\zwd* a house and land and provided him with food. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 There \zwd Hadad|Q5637555\zwd* found such great favor in the sight of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* that he gave to him in marriage \zwd the sister of \zwd Queen Tahpenes|Q12058221\zwd*|Q123126111\zwd*, his own wife. 
\v 20 And \zwd the sister of \zwd Tahpenes|Q12058221\zwd*|Q123126111\zwd* bore \zwd Hadad|Q5637555\zwd* a son named \zwd Genubath|Q9266968\zwd*. \zwd Tahpenes|Q12058221\zwd* herself weaned him in \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s palace, and \zwd Genubath|Q9266968\zwd* lived there among the sons of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 When \zwd Hadad|Q5637555\zwd* heard in Egypt that \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* had rested with his fathers and that \zwd Joab|id="Q1141064" t="2"\zwd*, the commander of the army, was dead, he said to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, “Let me go, that I may return to my own country.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 But \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* asked him, “What have you lacked here with me that you suddenly want to go back to your own country?” 
\b
\m “Nothing,” \zwd Hadad|Q5637555\zwd* replied, “but please let me go.” 
\s1 Rezon’s Hostility
\b
\m 
\v 23 And God raised up against \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* another adversary, \zwd Rezon the son of \zwd Eliada|\zwd*|Q13589419\zwd*, who had fled from his master, \zwd Hadadezer king of Zobah|\zwd*, 
\v 24 and had gathered men to himself. When \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* killed the Zobaites, \zwd Rezon|Q13589419\zwd* captained a band of raiders and went to Damascus, where they settled and gained control. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zwd Rezon|Q13589419\zwd* was Israel’s enemy throughout the days of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, adding to the trouble caused by \zwd Hadad|Q5637555\zwd*. So \zwd Rezon|Q13589419\zwd* ruled over Aram with hostility toward Israel. 
\s1 Jeroboam’s Rebellion
\b
\m 
\v 26 Now \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|Q313219\zwd* was an Ephraimite from Zeredah whose mother was a widow named \zwd Zeruah|Q8069663\zwd*. \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* was a servant of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, but he rebelled against \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd*, 
\v 27 and this is the account of his rebellion against \zwd the king|Q37085\zwd*. 
\b
\m \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* had built the supporting terraces \f + \fr 11:27 \ft Hebrew the Millo\f* and repaired the gap in the wall of the city of \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 28 Now \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* was a mighty man of valor. So when \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* noticed that \zwd the young man|Q313219\zwd* was industrious, he put him in charge of the whole labor force of the house of \zwd Joseph|id="Q145746" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 During that time, the prophet \zwd Ahijah the Shilonite|Q2590260\zwd* met \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* on the road as he was going out of Jerusalem. Now \zwd Ahijah|Q2590260\zwd* had wrapped himself in a new cloak, and \zwd the two of them|Q2590260,Q313219\zwd* were alone in the open field. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 And \zwd Ahijah|Q2590260\zwd* took hold of the new cloak he was wearing, tore it into twelve pieces, 
\v 31 and said to \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*, “Take ten pieces for yourself, for this is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘Behold, I will tear the kingdom out of the hand of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, and I will give you ten tribes. 
\v 32 But one tribe will remain for the sake of \zwd My servant David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* and for the sake of Jerusalem, the city I have chosen out of all the tribes of Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 For they have \f + \fr 11:33 \ft Hebrew; LXX, Syriac, and Vulgate he has; twice in this verse\f* forsaken Me to worship Ashtoreth the goddess of the Sidonians, Chemosh the god of the Moabites, and Milcom the god of the Ammonites. They have not walked in My ways, nor done what is right in My eyes, nor kept My statutes and judgments, as \zwd \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* did. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 Nevertheless, I will not take the whole kingdom out of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*’s hand, because I have made him ruler all the days of his life for the sake of \zwd David My servant|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, whom I chose because he kept My commandments and statutes. 
\v 35 But I will take ten tribes of the kingdom from the hand of \zwd his son|Q211663\zwd* and give them to you. 
\v 36 I will give one tribe to \zwd his son|Q211663\zwd*, so that \zwd My servant David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* will always have a lamp before Me in Jerusalem, the city where I chose to put My Name. 
\v 37 But as for you, I will take you, and you shall reign over all that your heart desires, and you will be king over Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 38 If you listen to all that I command you, walk in My ways, and do what is right in My sight in order to keep My statutes and commandments as \zwd My servant David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* did, then I will be with you. I will build you a lasting dynasty just as I built for \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, and I will give Israel to you. 
\v 39 Because of this, I will humble \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s descendants—but not forever.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 40 \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* therefore sought to kill \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*. But \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* arose and fled to Egypt, to \zwd Shishak king of Egypt|Q7499062\zwd*, where he remained until the death of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*. 
\s1 The Death of Solomon
\r (2 Chronicles 9:29–31)
\b
\m 
\v 41 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*—all that he did, as well as his wisdom—are they not written in the Book of the Acts of \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*? 
\v 42 Thus the time that \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* reigned in Jerusalem over all Israel was forty years. 
\b
\m 
\v 43 \zdate|Q37085:P39:Q43459871:P582\*And \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd* rested with his fathers and was buried in the city of \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. And \zwd his son Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* reigned in his place. 
\c 12
\s1 Rebellion against Rehoboam
\r (2 Chronicles 10:1–15)
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* went to Shechem, for all Israel had gone there to make him king. 
\v 2 When \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|Q313219\zwd* heard about this, he was still \f + \fr 12:2 \ft Or he remained\f* in Egypt where he had fled from \zwd King Solomon|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* and had been living ever since. 
\v 3 So they sent for \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*, and he and the whole assembly of Israel came to \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* and said, 
\v 4 “\zwd Your father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* put a heavy yoke on us. But now you must lighten the burden of \zwd your father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd*’s service and the heavy yoke he put on us, and we will serve you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* answered, “Go away for three days and then return to me.” So the people departed. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then \zwd King Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* consulted with the elders who had served \zwd his father Solomon|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* during his lifetime. “How do you advise me to respond to these people?” he asked. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 They replied, “If you will be a servant to these people and serve them this day, and if you will respond by speaking kind words to them, they will be your servants forever.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 But \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* rejected the advice of the elders; instead, he consulted the young men who had grown up with him and served him. 
\v 9 He asked them, “What message do you advise that we send back to these people who have spoken to me, saying, ‘Lighten the yoke \zwd your father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* put on us’?” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 The young men who had grown up with him replied, “This is how you should answer these people who said to you, ‘\zwd Your father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* made our yoke heavy, but you must make it lighter.’ This is what you should tell them: ‘My little finger is thicker than \zwd my father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd*’s waist! 
\v 11 Whereas \zwd my father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* burdened you with a heavy yoke, I will add to your yoke. Whereas \zwd my father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* scourged you with whips, I will scourge you with scorpions.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 After three days, \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* and all the people returned to \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd*, since \zwd the king|Q211663\zwd* had said, “Come back to me on the third day.” 
\v 13 And \zwd the king|Q211663\zwd* answered the people harshly. He rejected the advice of the elders 
\v 14 and spoke to them as the young men had advised, saying, “Whereas \zwd my father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* made your yoke heavy, I will add to your yoke. Whereas \zwd my father|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* scourged you with whips, I will scourge you with scorpions.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So \zwd the king|Q211663\zwd* did not listen to the people, and indeed this turn of events was from the LORD, to fulfill the word He had spoken to \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|Q313219\zwd* through \zwd Ahijah the Shilonite|Q2590260\zwd*. 
\s1 The Kingdom Divided
\r (2 Chronicles 10:16–19)
\b
\m 
\v 16 When all Israel saw that \zwd the king|Q211663\zwd* had refused to listen to them, they answered \zwd the king|Q211663\zwd*: 
\b
\q1 “What portion do we have in \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="3"\zwd*, 
\q2 and what inheritance in \zwd the son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q41370" t="3"\zwd*? 
\q1 To your tents, O Israel! 
\q2 Look now to your own house, O \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*!” 
\b
\m So the Israelites went home, 
\v 17 but \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* still reigned over the Israelites living in the cities of Judah. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd King Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* sent out \zwd Adoram|Q4057937\zwd*,\f + \fr 12:18 \ft Hebrew; some LXX manuscripts and Syriac Adoniram; see 1 Kings 4:6 and 1 Kings 5:14.\f* who was in charge of the forced labor, but all Israel stoned him to death. And \zwd King Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* mounted his chariot in haste and escaped to Jerusalem. 
\v 19 So to this day Israel has been in rebellion against the house of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\s1 Shemaiah’s Prophecy
\r (2 Chronicles 11:1–4)
\b
\m 
\v 20 \zdate|Q313219:P39:Q26835654:P580\*When all Israel heard that \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* had returned, they summoned him to the assembly and made him king over all Israel. Only the tribe of Judah followed the house of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 And when \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* arrived in Jerusalem, he mobilized the whole house of Judah and the tribe of Benjamin—180,000 chosen warriors—to fight against the house of Israel and restore the kingdom to \zwd Rehoboam son of \zwd Solomon|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd*|Q211663\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 But the word of God came to \zwd Shemaiah the man of God|Q2090113\zwd*: 
\v 23 “Tell \zwd Rehoboam son of \zwd Solomon|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* king of Judah|Q211663\zwd*, all the house of Judah and Benjamin, and the rest of the people 
\v 24 that this is what the LORD says: ‘You are not to go up and fight against your brothers, the Israelites. Each of you must return home, for this is My doing.’” 
\b
\m So they listened to the word of the LORD and turned back according to the word of the LORD. 
\s1 Jeroboam’s Idolatry
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* built Shechem in the hill country of Ephraim and lived there. And from there he went out and built Penuel.\f + \fr 12:25 \ft Penuel is a variant of Peniel; see Genesis 32:30.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* said in his heart, “Now the kingdom might revert to the house of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 27 If these people go up to offer sacrifices in the house of the LORD at Jerusalem, their hearts will return to their lord, \zwd Rehoboam king of Judah|Q211663\zwd*; then they will kill me and return to \zwd Rehoboam king of Judah|Q211663\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 After seeking advice, \zwd the king|Q211663\zwd* made two golden calves and said to the people,\f + \fr 12:28 \ft Hebrew to them\f* “Going up to Jerusalem is too much for you. Here, O Israel, are your gods, who brought you up out of the land of Egypt.” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 One calf he set up in Bethel, and the other in Dan. 
\v 30 And this thing became a sin; the people walked as far as Dan to worship before one of the calves.\f + \fr 12:30 \ft Likely reading of the original Hebrew text; MT the people walked to the one as far as Dan; LXX the people went to the one at Bethel and to the other as far as Dan\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 31 \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* also built shrines on the high places and appointed from every class of people priests who were not Levites. 
\v 32 And \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* ordained a feast on the fifteenth day of the eighth month,\f + \fr 12:32 \ft This feast was exactly one month after the annual Feast of Tabernacles in Judah; see Leviticus 23:34.\f* like the feast that was in Judah, and he offered sacrifices on the altar; he made this offering in Bethel to sacrifice to the calves he had set up, and he installed priests in Bethel for the high places he had set up. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 On the fifteenth day of the eighth month, a month of his own choosing, \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* offered sacrifices on the altar he had set up in Bethel. So he ordained a feast for the Israelites, offered sacrifices on the altar, and burned incense. 
\c 13
\s1 Jeroboam’s Hand Withers
\r (2 Kings 23:4–20; 2 Chronicles 34:3–7)
\m 
\v 1 Suddenly, as \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* was standing beside the altar to burn incense, there came \zwd a man of God from Judah|Q25491054\zwd* to Bethel by the word of the LORD. 
\v 2 And he cried out against the altar by the word of the LORD, “O altar, O altar, this is what the LORD says: ‘A son named \zwd Josiah|id="Q313228" t="2"\zwd* will be born to the house of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, and upon you he will sacrifice the priests of the high places who burn incense upon you, and human bones will be burned upon you.’”\f + \fr 13:2 \ft See 2 Kings 23:16.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 3 That day \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* gave a sign, saying, “The LORD has spoken this sign: ‘Surely the altar will be split apart, and the ashes upon it will be poured out.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Now when \zwd King Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*, who was at the altar in Bethel, heard the word that \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* had cried out against it, he stretched out his hand and said, “Seize him!” But the hand he stretched out toward him withered, so that he could not pull it back. 
\v 5 And the altar was split apart, and the ashes poured out, according to the sign that \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* had given by the word of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then \zwd the king|Q313219\zwd* responded to \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd*, “Intercede with the LORD your God and pray for me that my hand may be restored.” 
\b
\m So \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* interceded with the LORD, and \zwd the king|Q313219\zwd*’s hand was restored to him as it was before. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd the king|Q313219\zwd* said to \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd*, “Come home with me and refresh yourself, and I will give you a reward.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 But \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* replied, “If you were to give me half your possessions, I still would not go with you, nor would I eat bread or drink water in this place. 
\v 9 For this is what I was commanded by the word of the LORD: ‘You must not eat bread or drink water or return by the way you came.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* went another way and did not return by the way he had come to Bethel. 
\s1 The Old Prophet and the Man of God
\b
\m 
\v 11 Now \zwd a certain old prophet|\zwd* was living in Bethel, and his sons \f + \fr 13:11 \ft LXX, Syriac, and Vulgate; Hebrew son\f* came and told him all the deeds that \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* had done that day in Bethel. They also told \zwd their father|\zwd* the words that the man had spoken to \zwd the king|Q313219\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “Which way did he go?” \zwd their father|\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m And his sons showed him \f + \fr 13:12 \ft LXX; Hebrew had seen\f* the way taken by \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd*, who had come from Judah. 
\v 13 So \zwd the prophet|\zwd* said to his sons, “Saddle the donkey for me.” 
\b
\m Then they saddled the donkey for him, and he mounted it 
\v 14 and went after \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd*. He found him sitting under an oak tree \f + \fr 13:14 \ft Or a terebinth or a great tree\f* and asked, “Are you \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* who came from Judah?” 
\b
\m “I am,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So \zwd the prophet|\zwd* said to \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd*, “Come home with me and eat some bread.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 But the man replied, “I cannot go home with you, and I will not eat bread or drink water with you in this place. 
\v 17 For I have been told by the word of the LORD: ‘You must not eat bread or drink water there or return by the way you came.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd the prophet|\zwd* replied, “I too am a prophet like you, and an angel spoke to me by the word of the LORD, saying, ‘Bring him back with you to your house, so that he may eat bread and drink water.’” 
\b
\m \zwd The old prophet|\zwd* was lying to him, 
\v 19 but \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* went back with him, ate bread in his house, and drank water. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 While they were sitting at the table, the word of the LORD came to \zwd the prophet|\zwd* who had brought him back, 
\v 21 and \zwd the prophet|\zwd* cried out to \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* who had come from Judah, “This is what the LORD says: ‘Because you have defied the word of the LORD and have not kept the commandment that the LORD your God gave you, 
\v 22 but you went back and ate bread and drank water in the place where He told you not to do so, your body shall never reach the tomb of your fathers.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 And after \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* had finished eating and drinking, \zwd the old prophet|\zwd* who had brought him back saddled the donkey for him. 
\v 24 As he went on his way, a lion met him on the road and killed him, and his body was left lying in the road, with the donkey and the lion standing beside it. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 And there were men passing by who saw the body lying in the road with the lion standing beside it, and they went and reported this in the city where \zwd the old prophet|\zwd* lived. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 When \zwd the prophet|\zwd* who had brought him back from his journey heard this, he said, “It is \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* who disobeyed the command of the LORD. Therefore the LORD has delivered him to the lion, and it has mauled him and killed him, according to the word that the LORD had spoken to him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 Then \zwd the old prophet|\zwd* instructed his sons, “Saddle the donkey for me.” So they saddled it, 
\v 28 and he went and found the body lying in the road, with the donkey and the lion standing beside it. The lion had not eaten the body or mauled the donkey. 
\v 29 So \zwd the old prophet|\zwd* lifted up the body of \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd*, laid it on the donkey, and brought it back to his own city to mourn for him and bury him. 
\v 30 Then he laid the body in his own tomb, and they lamented over him, “Oh, \zwd my brother|\zwd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 After he had buried him, \zwd the prophet|\zwd* said to his sons, “When I die, you must bury me in the tomb where \zwd the man of God|Q25491054\zwd* is buried. Lay my bones beside his bones, 
\v 32 for the message that he cried out by the word of the LORD against the altar in Bethel and against all the shrines on the high places in the cities of Samaria will surely come to pass.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Even after these events, \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* did not repent of his evil ways, but again he appointed priests for the high places from every class of people. He ordained anyone who desired to be a priest of the high places. 
\v 34 And this was the sin of the house of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* that led to its extermination and destruction from the face of the earth. 
\c 14
\s1 Ahijah’s Prophecy against Jeroboam
\m 
\v 1 At that time \zwd Abijah son of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*|Q6817157\zwd* became ill, 
\v 2 and \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* said to \zwd his wife|Q18510709\zwd*, “Now get up, disguise yourself so they will not recognize you as \zwd my wife|\zwd*, and go to Shiloh. For \zwd Ahijah the prophet|Q2590260\zwd* is there; it was he who spoke about my kingship over this people. 
\v 3 Take with you ten loaves of bread, some cakes, and a jar of honey, and go to him. He will tell you what will become of \zwd the boy|\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 \zwd \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*’s wife|Q18510709\zwd* did as instructed; she arose and went to Shiloh and arrived at \zwd Ahijah|Q2590260\zwd*’s house. Now \zwd Ahijah|Q2590260\zwd* could not see, for his eyes were dim because of his age. 
\v 5 But the LORD had said to \zwd Ahijah|Q2590260\zwd*, “Behold, \zwd the wife of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*|Q18510709\zwd* is coming to ask you about \zwd her son|Q6817157\zwd*, for he is ill. You are to say such and such to her, because when she arrives, she will be disguised.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So when \zwd Ahijah|Q2590260\zwd* heard the sound of her feet entering the door, he said, “Come in, \zwd wife of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*|Q18510709\zwd*! Why are you disguised? For I have been sent to you with bad news. 
\v 7 Go, tell \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* that this is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘I raised you up from among the people and appointed you ruler over My people Israel. 
\v 8 I tore the kingdom away from the house of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* and gave it to you. But you have not been like \zwd My servant David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, who kept My commandments and followed Me with all his heart, doing only what was right in My eyes. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 You have done more evil than all who came before you. You have proceeded to make for yourself other gods and molten images to provoke Me, and you have flung Me behind your back. 
\v 10 Because of all this, behold, I am bringing disaster on the house of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*: 
\b
\q1 I will cut off from \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* every male,\f + \fr 14:10 \ft Literally (all) those who urinate against a wall\f* 
\q2 both slave and free, 
\q2 in Israel; 
\q1 I will burn up the house of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* 
\q2 as one burns up dung until it is gone! 
\q1 
\v 11 Anyone belonging to \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* who dies in the city 
\q2 will be eaten by dogs, 
\q1 and anyone who dies in the field 
\q2 will be eaten by the birds of the air.’ 
\qr For the LORD has spoken. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 As for you, get up and go home. When your feet enter the city, the child will die. 
\v 13 All Israel will mourn for him and bury him. For this is the only one belonging to \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* who will receive a proper burial,\f + \fr 14:13 \ft Literally who will come to the grave\f* because only in him has the LORD, the God of Israel, found any good in the house of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Moreover, the LORD will raise up for Himself \zwd a king|\zwd* over Israel who will cut off the house of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*. This is the day—yes, even today! 
\v 15 For the LORD will strike Israel as a reed is shaken in the water. He will uproot Israel from this good land that He gave their fathers, and He will scatter them beyond the Euphrates,\f + \fr 14:15 \ft Hebrew the River\f* because they have made their Asherah poles, provoking the LORD to anger. 
\v 16 So He will give Israel over on account of the sins \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* has committed and has caused Israel to commit.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*’s wife|Q18510709\zwd* got up and departed for Tirzah, and as soon as she stepped over the threshold of the house, \zwd the boy|Q6817157\zwd* died. 
\v 18 And they buried him, and all Israel mourned for him, according to the word that the LORD had spoken through \zwd His servant Ahijah the prophet|Q2590260\zwd*. 
\s1 Nadab Succeeds Jeroboam
\b
\m 
\v 19 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*, how he waged war and how he reigned, they are indeed written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And the length of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*’s reign was twenty-two years, and he rested with his fathers, and \zwd his son Nadab|Q313223\zwd* reigned in his place.\zdate|Q313219:P39:Q26835654:P582\* 
\s1 Rehoboam Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 12:13–14)
\b
\m 
\v 21 Meanwhile, \zwd Rehoboam son of \zwd Solomon|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd*|Q211663\zwd* reigned in Judah. He was forty-one years old when he became king, and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city the LORD had chosen from all the tribes of Israel in which to put His Name. His mother’s name was \zwd Naamah the Ammonite|Q865263\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And Judah did evil in the sight of the LORD, and by the sins they committed they provoked Him to jealous anger more than all their fathers had done. 
\v 23 They also built for themselves high places, sacred pillars, and Asherah poles on every high hill and under every green tree. 
\v 24 There were even male shrine prostitutes in the land. They imitated all the abominations of the nations the LORD had driven out before the Israelites. 
\s1 Shishak Raids Jerusalem
\r (2 Chronicles 12:1–12)
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zdate|Q211663:P39:Q10550167:P580+P5Y\*In the fifth year of \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd*’s reign, \zwd Shishak king of Egypt|Q7499062\zwd* attacked Jerusalem. 
\v 26 He seized the treasures of the house of the LORD and of the royal palace. He took everything, including all the gold shields that \zwd Solomon|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* had made. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 Then \zwd King Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* made bronze shields in their place and committed them to the care of the captains of the guard on duty at the entrance to the royal palace. 
\v 28 And whenever \zwd the king|Q211663\zwd* entered the house of the LORD, the guards would bear the shields, and later they would return them to the guardroom. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd*, along with all that he did, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 30 There was war between \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* and \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* throughout their days. 
\v 31 And \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* rested with his fathers and was buried with them in the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*; his mother’s name was \zwd Naamah the Ammonite|Q865263\zwd*. And his son \zwd Abijam|Q273517\zwd* \f + \fr 14:31 \ft Abijam is a variant of Abijah; some Hebrew manuscripts and LXX Abijah; see 2 Chronicles 12:16.\f* reigned in his place.\zdate|Q273517:P39:Q10550167:P580\* 
\c 15
\s1 Abijam Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 13:1–3)
\m 
\v 1 \zdate|Q313219:P39:Q43459871:P580+P18Y\*In the eighteenth year of the reign of \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|Q313219\zwd*, \zwd Abijam|Q273517\zwd* \f + \fr 15:1 \ft Abijam is a variant of Abijah; some Hebrew manuscripts and LXX Abijah; also in verses 3, 6, 7, and 8; see 2 Chronicles 12:16.\f* became king of Judah, 
\v 2 and he reigned in Jerusalem three years. His mother’s name was \zwd Maacah daughter of \zwd Abishalom|\zwd*|Q2903509\zwd*.\f + \fr 15:2 \ft Abishalom is a variant of Absalom; also in verse 10; see 2 Chronicles 11:20.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 3 And \zwd Abijam|Q273517\zwd* walked in all the sins that \zwd his father|Q211663\zwd* before him had committed, and his heart was not as fully devoted to the LORD his God as the heart of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* his forefather had been. 
\v 4 Nevertheless, for the sake of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, the LORD his God gave him a lamp in Jerusalem by raising up a son to succeed him and to make Jerusalem strong. 
\v 5 For \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* had done what was right in the eyes of the LORD and had not turned aside from anything the LORD commanded all the days of his life, except in the matter of \zwd Uriah the Hittite|Q71267\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 And there was war between the houses of \zwd Rehoboam|Q211663\zwd* \f + \fr 15:6 \ft Literally war between Rehoboam (most Hebrew manuscripts); some Hebrew manuscripts and Syriac war between Abijam (that is, Abijah); most LXX texts do not contain this verse.\f* and \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd* all the days of \zwd Abijam|Q273517\zwd*’s life.\f + \fr 15:6 \ft Literally of his life\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 7 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Abijam|Q273517\zwd*, along with all his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? And there was war between \zwd Abijam|Q273517\zwd* and \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And \zwd Abijam|Q273517\zwd* rested with his fathers and was buried in the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, and \zwd his son Asa|Q313415\zwd* reigned in his place.\zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580\* 
\s1 Asa Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 14:1–15; 2 Chronicles 15:8–19)
\b
\m 
\v 9 \zdate|Q313219:P39:Q43459871:P580+P20Y\*In the twentieth year of \zwd Jeroboam|Q313219\zwd*’s reign over Israel, \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd* became king of Judah, 
\v 10 and he reigned in Jerusalem forty-one years. His grandmother’s \f + \fr 15:10 \ft Or The queen mother’s; Hebrew His mother’s; see verses 2 and 13.\f* name was \zwd Maacah daughter of \zwd Abishalom|\zwd*|Q2903509\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 And \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd* did what was right in the eyes of the LORD, as \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* had done. 
\v 12 He banished the male shrine prostitutes from the land and removed all the idols that his fathers had made. 
\v 13 He also removed \zwd his grandmother Maacah|Q2903509\zwd* from her position as queen mother because she had made a detestable Asherah pole. \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd* chopped down the pole and burned it in the Kidron Valley. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 The high places were not removed, but \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s heart was fully devoted to the LORD all his days. 
\v 15 And he brought into the house of the LORD the silver and gold and the articles that he and his father had dedicated. 
\s1 War between Asa and Baasha
\r (2 Chronicles 16:1–6)
\b
\m 
\v 16 Now there was war between \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd* and \zwd Baasha king of Israel|Q313224\zwd* throughout their days. 
\v 17 \zwd Baasha king of Israel|Q313224\zwd* went to war against Judah and fortified Ramah to prevent anyone from leaving or entering the territory of \zwd Asa king of Judah|Q313415\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd* withdrew all the silver and gold that remained in the treasuries of the house of the LORD and the royal palace. He entrusted it to his servants and sent them with this message to \zwd Ben-hadad son of \zwd Tabrimmon|id="Q6202803" t="2"\zwd*|Q816397\zwd*, the son of \zwd Hezion|id="Q5750060" t="2"\zwd*, the king of Aram, who was ruling in Damascus: 
\v 19 “Let there be a treaty \f + \fr 15:19 \ft Forms of the Hebrew berit are translated in most passages as covenant; twice in this verse.\f* between me and you as there was between \zwd my father|\zwd* and \zwd your father|\zwd*. See, I have sent you a gift of silver and gold. Now go and break your treaty with \zwd Baasha king of Israel|Q313224\zwd*, so that he will withdraw from me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And \zwd Ben-hadad|Q816397\zwd* listened to \zwd King Asa|Q313415\zwd* and sent the commanders of his armies against the cities of Israel, conquering Ijon, Dan, Abel-beth-maacah, and the whole land of Naphtali, including the region of Chinnereth. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 When \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* learned of this, he stopped fortifying Ramah and withdrew to Tirzah. 
\v 22 Then \zwd King Asa|Q313415\zwd* summoned all the men of Judah,\f + \fr 15:22 \ft Or Then King Asa made a proclamation throughout all Judah\f* with no exceptions, and they carried away the stones of Ramah and the timbers \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* had used for building. And with these materials \zwd King Asa|Q313415\zwd* built up Geba of Benjamin, as well as Mizpah. 
\s1 Jehoshaphat Succeeds Asa
\r (2 Chronicles 17:1–19)
\b
\m 
\v 23 Now the rest of the acts of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*, along with all his might, all his accomplishments, and the cities he built, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? In his old age, however, he became diseased in his feet. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 And \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd* rested with his fathers and was buried with them in the city of \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, and \zwd his son Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* reigned in his place.\zdate|Q126689:P39:Q10550167:P580\* 
\s1 Nadab Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580+P2Y\*In the second year of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Nadab son of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*|Q313223\zwd* became king of Israel, and he reigned two years. 
\v 26 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD and walked in the way of \zwd his father|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd* and in his sin, which he had caused Israel to commit. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 \zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580+P3Y\*Then \zwd Baasha son of \zwd Ahijah|id="Q114841071" t="2"\zwd*|Q313224\zwd* of the house of Issachar conspired against \zwd Nadab|Q313223\zwd*, and \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* struck him down at Gibbethon of the Philistines while \zwd Nadab|Q313223\zwd* and all Israel were besieging the city. 
\v 28 In the third year of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* killed \zwd Nadab|Q313223\zwd* and reigned in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 As soon as \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* became king, he struck down the entire household of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*. He did not leave to \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd* anyone who breathed, but destroyed them all according to the word that the LORD had spoken through \zwd His servant Ahijah the Shilonite|Q2590260\zwd*, 
\v 30 because of the sins \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd* had committed and had caused Israel to commit, and because he had provoked the LORD, the God of Israel, to anger. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Nadab|Q313223\zwd*, along with all his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\v 32 And there was war between \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd* and \zwd Baasha king of Israel|Q313224\zwd* throughout their days. 
\s1 Baasha Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 33 \zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580+P3Y\*In the third year of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Baasha son of \zwd Ahijah|id="Q114841071" t="2"\zwd*|Q313224\zwd* became king of all Israel, and he reigned in Tirzah twenty-four years. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 And \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* did evil in the sight of the LORD and walked in the way of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd* and in his sin, which he had caused Israel to commit. 
\c 16
\s1 Jehu’s Prophecy against Baasha
\m 
\v 1 Then the word of the LORD came to \zwd Jehu son of \zwd Hanani|id="Q10293838" t="2"\zwd*|Q1686571\zwd* against \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd*, saying: 
\v 2 “Even though I lifted you out of the dust and made you ruler over My people Israel, you have walked in the way of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd* and have caused My people Israel to sin and to provoke Me to anger by their sins. 
\v 3 So now I will consume \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* and his house, and I will make your house like that of \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*: 
\b
\q1 
\v 4 Anyone belonging to \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* who dies in the city 
\q2 will be eaten by dogs, 
\q1 and anyone who dies in the field 
\q2 will be eaten by the birds of the air.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd*, along with his accomplishments and might, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\v 6 And \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* rested with his fathers and was buried in Tirzah, and \zwd his son Elah|Q319020\zwd* reigned in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Moreover, the word of the LORD came through the prophet \zwd Jehu son of \zwd Hanani|id="Q10293838" t="2"\zwd*|Q1686571\zwd* against \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* and his house, because of all the evil he had done in the sight of the LORD, provoking Him to anger with the work of his hands and becoming like the house of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*, and also because \zwd Baasha|Q313224\zwd* had struck down the house of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*. 
\s1 Elah Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580+P26Y\*In the twenty-sixth year of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Elah son of \zwd Baasha|id="Q313224" t="2"\zwd*|Q319020\zwd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Tirzah two years. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 However, while \zwd Elah|Q319020\zwd* was in Tirzah getting drunk in the house of \zwd Arza the steward of his household|\zwd* there, \zwd \zwd Elah|Q319020\zwd*’s servant Zimri|Q313230\zwd*, the commander of half his chariots, conspired against him. 
\v 10 \zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580+P27Y\*So in the twenty-seventh year of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Zimri|Q313230\zwd* went in, struck \zwd Elah|Q319020\zwd* down, and killed him. And \zwd Zimri|Q313230\zwd* reigned in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 As soon as \zwd Zimri|Q313230\zwd* began to reign and was seated on the throne, he struck down the entire household of \zwd Baasha|id="Q313224" t="2"\zwd*. He did not leave a single male, whether a kinsman or friend. 
\v 12 So \zwd Zimri|Q313230\zwd* destroyed the entire household of \zwd Baasha|id="Q313224" t="2"\zwd*, according to the word that the LORD had spoken against \zwd Baasha|id="Q313224" t="2"\zwd* through \zwd Jehu the prophet|Q1686571\zwd*. 
\v 13 This happened because of all the sins \zwd Baasha|id="Q313224" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his son Elah|Q319020\zwd* had committed and had caused Israel to commit, provoking the LORD, the God of Israel, to anger with their worthless idols. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Elah|Q319020\zwd*, along with all his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\s1 Zimri Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 15 \zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580+P27Y\*In the twenty-seventh year of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Zimri|Q313230\zwd* reigned in Tirzah for seven days. Now the troops were encamped against Gibbethon of the Philistines, 
\v 16 and the people in the camp heard that \zwd Zimri|Q313230\zwd* had conspired against \zwd the king|Q319020\zwd* and struck him down. So there in the camp that very day, all Israel proclaimed \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd*, the commander of the army, king over Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd* and all the Israelites marched up from Gibbethon and besieged Tirzah. 
\v 18 When \zwd Zimri|Q313230\zwd* saw that the city was captured, he entered the citadel of the royal palace and burned it down upon himself. So he died 
\v 19 because of the sins he had committed, doing evil in the sight of the LORD and following the example of \zwd Jeroboam|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd* and the sin he had committed and had caused Israel to commit. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Zimri|Q313230\zwd* and the treason he committed, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\s1 Omri Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 21 At that time the people of Israel were divided: Half of the people supported \zwd Tibni son of \zwd Ginath|id="Q114906247" t="2"\zwd*|Q887994\zwd* as king, and half supported \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd*. 
\v 22 But the followers of \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd* proved stronger than those of \zwd Tibni son of \zwd Ginath|id="Q114906247" t="2"\zwd*|Q887994\zwd*. So \zwd Tibni|Q887994\zwd* died and \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd* became king. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 \zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580+P31Y\*In the thirty-first year of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd* became king of Israel, and he reigned twelve years, six of them in Tirzah. 
\v 24 He bought the hill of Samaria from \zwd Shemer|Q133454476\zwd* for two talents of silver \f + \fr 16:24 \ft 2 talents is approximately 151 pounds or 68.4 kilograms of silver.\f* and built a city there, calling it Samaria after the name of \zwd Shemer|Q133454476\zwd*, who had owned the hill. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 But \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd* did evil in the sight of the LORD and acted more wickedly than all who were before him. 
\v 26 For he walked in all the ways of \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd* and in his sins, which he caused Israel to commit, provoking the LORD, the God of Israel, to anger with their worthless idols. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd*, along with his accomplishments and the might he exercised, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\b
\m 
\v 28 And \zwd Omri|Q313221\zwd* rested with his fathers and was buried in Samaria, and \zwd his son Ahab|Q235901\zwd* reigned in his place. 
\s1 Ahab Reigns in Israel, Marries Jezebel
\b
\m 
\v 29 \zdate|Q313415:P39:Q10550167:P580+P38Y\*In the thirty-eighth year of \zwd Asa|Q313415\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Ahab son of \zwd Omri|id="Q313221" t="2"\zwd*|Q235901\zwd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria twenty-two years. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 However, \zwd Ahab son of \zwd Omri|id="Q313221" t="2"\zwd*|Q235901\zwd* did evil in the sight of the LORD, more than all who were before him. 
\v 31 And as if it were not enough for him to walk in the sins of \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*, he even married \zwd Jezebel the daughter of \zwd Ethbaal king of the Sidonians|id="Q1277966" t="2"\zwd*|Q721295\zwd*, and he then proceeded to serve and worship Baal. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 First, \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* set up an altar for Baal in the temple of Baal that he had built in Samaria. 
\v 33 Then he set up an Asherah pole. Thus \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* did more to provoke the LORD, the God of Israel, to anger than all the kings of Israel before him. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 In \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*’s days, \zwd Hiel the Bethelite|Q65176077\zwd* rebuilt Jericho. At the cost of \zwd Abiram his firstborn|Q114008399\zwd* he laid its foundation, and at the cost of \zwd Segub his youngest|Q16258370\zwd* he set up its gates, according to the word that the LORD had spoken through \zwd Joshua son of \zwd Nun|id="Q3344859" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q7734" t="2"\zwd*.\f + \fr 16:34 \ft See Joshua 6:26.\f* 
\c 17
\s1 The Ravens Feed Elijah
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Elijah the Tishbite|Q133507\zwd*, who was among the settlers of Gilead,\f + \fr 17:1 \ft Or who was from Tishbe in Gilead\f* said to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*, “As surely as the LORD, the God of Israel, lives, before whom I stand, there will be neither dew nor rain in these years except at my word!” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 Then a revelation from the LORD came to \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*: 
\v 3 “Leave here, turn eastward, and hide yourself by the Brook of Cherith, east of the Jordan.\f + \fr 17:3 \ft Or the Cherith Ravine, near the Jordan; also in verse 5\f* 
\v 4 And you are to drink from the brook, and I have commanded the ravens to feed you there.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* did what the LORD had told him, and he went and lived by the Brook of Cherith, east of the Jordan. 
\v 6 The ravens would bring him bread and meat in the morning and evening, and he would drink from the brook. 
\v 7 Some time later, however, the brook dried up because there had been no rain in the land. 
\s1 The Widow of Zarephath
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then the word of the LORD came to \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*: 
\v 9 “Get up and go to Zarephath of Sidon, and stay there. Behold, I have commanded \zwd a widow|Q6605722\zwd* there to provide for you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* got up and went to Zarephath. When he arrived at the city gate, there was \zwd a widow|Q6605722\zwd* gathering sticks. \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* called to her and said, “Please bring me a little water in a cup, so that I may drink.” 
\v 11 And as she was going to get it, he called to her and said, “Please bring me a piece of bread.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 But she replied, “As surely as the LORD your God lives, I have no bread—only a handful of flour in a jar and a little oil in a jug. Look, I am gathering a couple of sticks to take home and prepare a meal for myself and my son, so that we may eat it and die.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 “Do not be afraid,” \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* said to her. “Go and do as you have said. But first make me a small cake of bread from what you have, and bring it out to me. Afterward, make some for yourself and your son, 
\v 14 for this is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘The jar of flour will not be exhausted and the jug of oil will not run dry until the day the LORD sends rain upon the face of the earth.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So she went and did according to the word of \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*, and there was food every day for \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* and the woman and her household. 
\v 16 The jar of flour was not exhausted and the jug of oil did not run dry, according to the word that the LORD had spoken through \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*. 
\s1 Elijah Raises the Widow’s Son
\b
\m 
\v 17 Later, \zwd the son of \zwd the woman who owned the house|Q6605722\zwd*|\zwd* became ill, and his sickness grew worse and worse, until no breath remained in him. 
\v 18 “O man of God,” said \zwd the woman|Q6605722\zwd* to \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*, “what have you done to me? Have you come to remind me of my iniquity and cause the death of \zwd my son|\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* said to her, “Give me \zwd your son|\zwd*.” 
\b
\m So he took him from her arms, carried him to the upper room where he was staying, and laid him on his own bed. 
\v 20 Then he cried out to the LORD, “O LORD my God, have You also brought tragedy on \zwd this widow|Q6605722\zwd* who has opened her home to me, by causing \zwd her son|\zwd* to die?” 
\v 21 Then he stretched himself out over \zwd the child|\zwd* three times and cried out to the LORD, “O LORD my God, please let \zwd this boy|\zwd*’s life return to him!” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And the LORD listened to the voice of \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*, and \zwd the child|\zwd*’s life returned to him, and he lived. 
\v 23 Then \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* took \zwd the child|\zwd*, brought him down from the upper room into the house, and gave him to \zwd his mother|Q6605722\zwd*. “Look, \zwd your son|\zwd* is alive,” \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* declared. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \zwd the woman|Q6605722\zwd* said to \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*, “Now I know that you are a man of God and that the word of the LORD from your mouth is truth.” 
\c 18
\s1 Elijah’s Message to Ahab
\m 
\v 1 After a long time, in the third year of the drought,\f + \fr 18:1 \ft Literally And the days were many, and in the third year,\f* the word of the LORD came to \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*: “Go and present yourself to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*, and I will send rain upon the face of the earth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 So \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* went to present himself to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*. The famine was severe in Samaria, 
\v 3 and \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* summoned \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd*, who was in charge of the palace. 
\b
\m (Now \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd* greatly feared the LORD, 
\v 4 for when \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* had slaughtered the prophets of the LORD, \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd* had taken a hundred prophets and hidden them, fifty men per cave, providing them with food and water.) 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* said to \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd*, “Go throughout the land to every spring and every valley. Perhaps we will find grass to keep the horses and mules alive so that we will not have to destroy any livestock.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So they divided the land to explore. \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* went one way by himself, and \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd* went the other way by himself. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now as \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd* went on his way, \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* suddenly met him. When \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd* recognized him, he fell facedown and said, “Is it you, \zwd my lord Elijah|Q133507\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 “It is I,” he answered. “Go tell your master, ‘\zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* is here!’” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd* replied, “How have I sinned, that you are handing \zwd your servant|Q7074556\zwd* over to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* to put me to death? 
\v 10 As surely as the LORD your God lives, there is no nation or kingdom where \zwd my lord|Q235901\zwd* has not sent someone to search for you. When they said, ‘He is not here,’ he made that kingdom or nation swear that they had not found you. 
\v 11 And now you say, ‘Go tell your master that \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* is here!’ 
\b
\m 
\v 12 I do not know where the Spirit of the LORD may carry you off when I leave you. Then when I go and tell \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* and he does not find you, he will kill me. But I, \zwd your servant|Q7074556\zwd*, have feared the LORD from my youth. 
\v 13 Was it not reported to \zwd my lord|\zwd* what I did when \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* slaughtered the prophets of the LORD? I hid a hundred prophets of the LORD, fifty men per cave, and I provided them with food and water. 
\v 14 And now you say, ‘Go tell your lord that \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* is here!’ He will kill me!” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* said, “As surely as the LORD of Hosts lives, before whom I stand, I will present myself to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* today.” 
\s1 Elijah on Mount Carmel
\b
\m 
\v 16 So \zwd Obadiah|Q7074556\zwd* went to inform \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*, who went to meet \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*. 
\v 17 When \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* saw \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*, he said to him, “Is that you, O troubler of Israel?” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 “I have not troubled Israel,” \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* replied, “but you and \zwd your father|\zwd*’s house have, for you have forsaken the commandments of the LORD and have followed the Baals. 
\v 19 Now summon all Israel to meet me on Mount Carmel, along with the four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal and the four hundred prophets of Asherah who eat at \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd*’s table.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* summoned all the Israelites and assembled the prophets on Mount Carmel. 
\v 21 Then \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* approached all the people and said, “How long will you waver between two opinions? If the LORD is God, follow Him. But if Baal is God, follow him.” 
\b
\m But the people did not answer a word. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* said to the people, “I am the only remaining prophet of the LORD, but Baal has four hundred and fifty prophets. 
\v 23 Get two bulls for us. Let the prophets of Baal choose one bull for themselves, cut it into pieces, and place it on the wood but not light the fire. And I will prepare the other bull and place it on the wood but not light the fire. 
\v 24 Then you may call on the name of your god, and I will call on the name of the LORD. The God who answers by fire, He is God.” 
\b
\m And all the people answered, “What you say is good.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* said to the prophets of Baal, “Since you are so numerous, choose for yourselves one bull and prepare it first. Then call on the name of your god, but do not light the fire.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 And they took the bull that was given them, prepared it, and called on the name of Baal from morning until noon, shouting, “O Baal, answer us!” 
\b
\m But there was no sound, and no one answered as they leaped around the altar they had made. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 At noon \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* began to taunt them, saying, “Shout louder, for he is a god! Perhaps he is deep in thought, or occupied, or on a journey. Perhaps he is sleeping and must be awakened!” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 So they shouted louder and cut themselves with knives and lances, as was their custom, until the blood gushed over them. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 Midday passed, and they kept on raving until the time of the evening sacrifice. But there was no response; no one answered, no one paid attention. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Then \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* said to all the people, “Come near to me.” So all the people approached him, and he repaired the altar of the LORD that had been torn down. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 And \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* took twelve stones, one for each tribe of the sons of \zwd Jacob|id="Q289957" t="2"\zwd*, to whom the word of the LORD had come and said, “Israel shall be your name.” 
\v 32 And with the stones, \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* built an altar in the name of the LORD. Then he dug a trench around the altar large enough to hold two seahs of seed.\f + \fr 18:32 \ft 2 seahs is approximately 13.2 dry quarts or 14.6 liters (probably about 25.4 pounds or 11.5 kilograms of seed).\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Next, he arranged the wood, cut up the bull, placed it on the wood, 
\v 34 and said, “Fill four waterpots and pour the water on the offering and on the wood.”\f + \fr 18:34 \ft Some texts continue verse 33 to this point.\f* 
\b
\m “Do it a second time,” he said, and they did it a second time. 
\b
\m “Do it a third time,” he said, and they did it a third time. 
\b
\m 
\v 35 So the water ran down around the altar and even filled the trench. 
\s1 Elijah’s Prayer
\b
\m 
\v 36 At the time of the evening sacrifice, \zwd Elijah the prophet|Q133507\zwd* approached the altar and said, “O LORD, God of \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*, and \zwd Israel|id="Q289957" t="2"\zwd*, let it be known this day that You are God in Israel and that I am Your servant and have done all these things at Your command. 
\v 37 Answer me, O LORD! Answer me, so that this people will know that You, the LORD, are God, and that You have turned their hearts back again.” 
\b
\m 
\v 38 Then the fire of the LORD fell and consumed the sacrifice, the wood, the stones, and the dust, and it licked up the water in the trench. 
\b
\m 
\v 39 When all the people saw this, they fell facedown and said, “The LORD, He is God! The LORD, He is God!” 
\b
\m 
\v 40 Then \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* ordered them, “Seize the prophets of Baal! Do not let a single one escape.” So they seized them, and \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* brought them down to the Kishon Valley and slaughtered them there. 
\s1 The LORD Sends Rain
\b
\m 
\v 41 And \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* said to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*, “Go up, eat and drink, for there is the sound of a heavy rain.” 
\b
\m 
\v 42 So \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* went up to eat and drink. But \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* climbed to the summit of Carmel, bent down on the ground, and put his face between his knees. 
\v 43 “Go and look toward the sea,” he said to \zwd his servant|\zwd*. 
\b
\m So \zwd the servant|\zwd* went and looked, and he said, “There is nothing there.” 
\b
\m Seven times \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* said, “Go back.” 
\b
\m 
\v 44 On the seventh time \zwd the servant|\zwd* reported, “There is a cloud as small as a man’s hand rising from the sea.” 
\b
\m And \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* replied, “Go and tell \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*, ‘Prepare your chariot and go down before the rain stops you.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 45 Meanwhile, the sky grew dark with clouds and wind, and a heavy rain began to fall. So \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* rode away and went to Jezreel. 
\b
\m 
\v 46 And the hand of the LORD came upon \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*, and he tucked his cloak into his belt \f + \fr 18:46 \ft Hebrew he girded up his loins\f* and ran ahead of \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* all the way to Jezreel. 
\c 19
\s1 Elijah Flees from Jezebel
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* told \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* everything that \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* had done and how he had killed all the prophets with the sword. 
\v 2 So \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* sent a messenger to \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*, saying, “May the gods deal with me, and ever so severely, if by this time tomorrow I have not made your life like the lives of those you killed!” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 And \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* was afraid and ran for his life. When he came to Beersheba in Judah, he left \zwd his servant|\zwd* there, 
\v 4 while he himself went a day’s journey into the wilderness. He sat down under a broom tree and prayed that he might die. “I have had enough, LORD,” he said. “Take my life, for I am no better than my fathers.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then he lay down under the broom tree and fell asleep. 
\b
\m Suddenly an angel touched him and said, “Get up and eat.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 And he looked around, and there by his head was a cake of bread baked over hot coals, and a jar of water. So he ate and drank and lay down again. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 A second time the angel of the LORD returned and touched him, saying, “Get up and eat, or the journey will be too much for you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So he got up and ate and drank. And strengthened by that food, he walked forty days and forty nights until he reached Horeb,\f + \fr 19:8 \ft That is, Mount Sinai, or possibly a mountain in the range containing Mount Sinai\f* the mountain of God. 
\s1 The LORD Speaks to Elijah at Horeb
\b
\m 
\v 9 There \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* entered a cave and spent the night. And the word of the LORD came to him, saying, “What are you doing here, \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “I have been very zealous for the LORD, the God of Hosts,” he replied, “but the Israelites have forsaken Your covenant, torn down Your altars, and killed Your prophets with the sword. I am the only one left, and they are seeking my life as well.”\f + \fr 19:10 \ft Cited in Romans 11:3; here and in verse 14\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then the LORD said, “Go out and stand on the mountain before the LORD. Behold, the LORD is about to pass by.” 
\b
\m And a great and mighty wind tore into the mountains and shattered the rocks before the LORD, but the LORD was not in the wind. 
\b
\m After the wind there was an earthquake, but the LORD was not in the earthquake. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 After the earthquake there was a fire, but the LORD was not in the fire. 
\b
\m And after the fire came a still, small voice. 
\v 13 When \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* heard it, he wrapped his face in his cloak and went out and stood at the mouth of the cave. Suddenly a voice came to him and said, “What are you doing here, \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 “I have been very zealous for the LORD, the God of Hosts,” he replied, “but the Israelites have forsaken Your covenant, torn down Your altars, and killed Your prophets with the sword. I am the only one left, and they are seeking my life as well.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then the LORD said to him, “Go back by the way you came, and go to the Desert of Damascus. When you arrive, you are to anoint \zwd Hazael|Q127170\zwd* as king over Aram. 
\v 16 You are also to anoint \zwd Jehu son \f + \fr 19:16 \ft Or grandson; see 2 Kings 9:14.\f* of \zwd Nimshi|\zwd*|Q313225\zwd* as king over Israel and \zwd Elisha son of \zwd Shaphat|id="Q114685996" t="2"\zwd*|Q206238\zwd* from Abel-meholah to succeed you as prophet. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd Jehu|Q313225\zwd* will put to death whoever escapes the sword of \zwd Hazael|Q127170\zwd*, and \zwd Elisha|Q206238\zwd* will put to death whoever escapes the sword of \zwd Jehu|Q313225\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Nevertheless, I have reserved seven thousand in Israel—all whose knees have not bowed to Baal \f + \fr 19:18 \ft Cited in Romans 11:4\f* and whose mouths have not kissed him.” 
\s1 The Call of Elisha
\b
\m 
\v 19 So \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* departed and found \zwd Elisha son of \zwd Shaphat|id="Q114685996" t="2"\zwd*|Q206238\zwd*. He was plowing with twelve teams of oxen, and he was with the twelfth team. \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* passed by him and threw his cloak around him. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So \zwd Elisha|Q206238\zwd* left the oxen, ran after \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*, and said, “Please let me kiss \zwd my father|Q114685996\zwd* and \zwd mother|\zwd* goodbye, and then I will follow you.” 
\b
\m “Go on back,” \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* replied, “for what have I done to you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So \zwd Elisha|Q206238\zwd* turned back from him, took his pair of oxen, and slaughtered them. Using the oxen’s equipment for fuel, he cooked the meat and gave it to the people, and they ate. Then he set out to follow and serve \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd*. 
\c 20
\s1 Ben-hadad Attacks Samaria
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Ben-hadad king of Aram|Q717814\zwd* assembled his entire army. Accompanied by \zwd thirty-two kings|\zwd* with their horses and chariots, he marched up, besieged Samaria, and waged war against it. 
\v 2 Then he sent messengers into the city to \zwd Ahab king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*, 
\v 3 saying, “This is what \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* says:\f + \fr 20:3 \ft Some texts break verse 2 and begin verse 3 at this point.\f* ‘Your silver and gold are mine, and your best wives and children are mine!’” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 And \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* replied, “Just as you say, \zwd my lord the king|Q717814\zwd*: I am yours, along with all that I have.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 The messengers came back and said, “This is what \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* says: ‘I have sent to you to demand your silver, your gold, your wives, and your children. 
\v 6 But about this time tomorrow I will send my servants to search your palace and the houses of your servants. They will seize and carry away all that is precious to you.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* summoned all the elders of the land and said, “Please take note and see that this man is looking for trouble, for when he demanded my wives, my children, my silver, and my gold, I did not deny him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And the elders and the people all said, “Do not listen to him or consent to his terms.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* answered the messengers of \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd*, “Tell \zwd my lord the king|Q717814\zwd*, ‘All that you demanded of your servant the first time I will do, but this thing I cannot do.’” 
\b
\m So the messengers departed and relayed the message to \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* sent another message to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*: “May the gods deal with me, and ever so severely, if enough dust remains of Samaria for each of my men to have a handful.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 And \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* replied, “Tell him: ‘The one putting on his armor should not boast like one taking it off.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* received this message while he and \zwd the kings|\zwd* were drinking in their tents,\f + \fr 20:12 \ft Or in Succoth; also in verse 16\f* and he said to his servants, “Take your positions.” So they stationed themselves against the city. 
\s1 Ahab Defeats Ben-hadad
\b
\m 
\v 13 Meanwhile \zwd a prophet|\zwd* approached \zwd Ahab king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* and declared, “This is what the LORD says: ‘Do you see this entire great army? Behold, I will deliver it into your hand this very day, and you will know that I am the LORD.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 “By whom?” \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m And \zwd the prophet|\zwd* replied, “This is what the LORD says: ‘By the young officers of the district governors.’” 
\b
\m “Who will start the battle?” asked \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*. 
\b
\m “You will,” answered \zwd the prophet|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* assembled the young officers of the district governors, and there were 232 men. And after them, he assembled the rest of the Israelite troops, 7,000 in all. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 They marched out at noon while \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* and \zwd the 32 kings|\zwd* allied with him were in their tents getting drunk. 
\v 17 And the young officers of the district governors marched out first. 
\b
\m Now \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* had sent out scouts, who reported to him, “Men are marching out of Samaria.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 “If they have marched out in peace,” he said, “take them alive. Even if they have marched out for war, take them alive.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Meanwhile, these young officers of the district governors marched out of the city, with the army behind them, 
\v 20 and each one struck down his opponent. So the Arameans fled, with the Israelites in pursuit. But \zwd Ben-hadad king of Aram|Q717814\zwd* escaped on horseback with the cavalry. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* marched out and attacked the horses and chariots, inflicting a great slaughter on the Arameans. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Afterward, \zwd the prophet|\zwd* approached \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* and said, “Go and strengthen your position, and take note what you must do, for in the spring \f + \fr 20:22 \ft Literally at the turn of the year; similarly in verse 26\f* \zwd the king of Aram|Q717814\zwd* will come up against you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Meanwhile, the servants of \zwd the king of Aram|Q717814\zwd* said to him, “Their gods are gods of the hills. That is why they prevailed over us. Instead, we should fight them on the plains; surely then we will prevail. 
\v 24 So do this: Dismiss all the kings from their positions and replace them with other officers. 
\v 25 And you must raise an army like the one you have lost—horse for horse and chariot for chariot—so we can fight the Israelites on the plain, where we will surely prevail.” 
\b
\m And \zwd the king|Q717814\zwd* approved their plan and acted accordingly. 
\s1 Another War with Ben-hadad
\b
\m 
\v 26 In the spring, \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* mobilized the Arameans and went up to Aphek to fight against Israel. 
\v 27 The Israelites also mobilized, gathered supplies, and marched out to meet them. 
\b
\m The Israelites camped before them like two small flocks of goats, while the Arameans covered the countryside. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Then the man of God approached \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* and said, “This is what the LORD says: ‘Because the Arameans have said that the LORD is a god of the hills and not of the valleys, I will deliver all this great army into your hand. Then you will know that I am the LORD.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 For seven days the armies camped opposite each other, and on the seventh day the battle ensued, and the Israelites struck down the Arameans—a hundred thousand foot soldiers in one day. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 The rest of them fled into the city of Aphek, where the wall fell on twenty-seven thousand of the remaining men. \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* also fled to the city and hid in an inner room. 
\s1 Ahab Spares Ben-hadad
\b
\m 
\v 31 Then the servants of \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* said to him, “Look now, we have heard that the kings of the house of Israel are merciful. Let us go out to \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* with sackcloth around our waists and ropes around our heads. Perhaps he will spare your life.” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 So with sackcloth around their waists and ropes around their heads, they went to \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* and said, “\zwd Your servant Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* says, ‘Please spare my life.’” 
\b
\m And \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd* answered, “Is he still alive? He is my brother.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Now the men were looking for a sign of hope, and they quickly grasped at this word and replied, “Yes, \zwd your brother Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd*.” 
\b
\m “Go and get him!” said \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*. 
\b
\m Then \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* came out, and \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* had him come up into his chariot. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 \zwd Ben-hadad|Q717814\zwd* said to him, “I will restore the cities \zwd my father|id="Q816397" t="2"\zwd* took from \zwd your father|id="Q313224" t="2"\zwd*; you may set up your own marketplaces in Damascus, as \zwd my father|\zwd* did in Samaria.” 
\b
\m “By this treaty \f + \fr 20:34 \ft Forms of the Hebrew berit are translated in most passages as covenant; twice in this verse.\f* I release you,” \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* replied. So he made a treaty with him and sent him away. 
\s1 A Prophet Reproves Ahab
\b
\m 
\v 35 Meanwhile, by the word of the LORD, one of the sons of the prophets said to his companion, “Strike me, please!” 
\b
\m But the man refused to strike him. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 Then the prophet said to him, “Because you have not obeyed the voice of the LORD, as soon as you depart from me a lion will kill you.” 
\b
\m And when he left, a lion found him and killed him. 
\b
\m 
\v 37 Then the prophet found another man and said, “Strike me, please!” 
\b
\m So the man struck him and wounded him, 
\v 38 and the prophet went and waited on the road for \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*, disguising himself with a bandage over his eyes. 
\b
\m 
\v 39 As \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd* passed by, he cried out to \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*: “Your servant had marched out into the middle of the battle, when suddenly a man came over with a captive and told me, ‘Guard this man! If he goes missing for any reason, your life will be exchanged for his life, or you will weigh out a talent of silver.\f + \fr 20:39 \ft A talent is approximately 75.4 pounds or 34.2 kilograms of silver.\f*’ 
\v 40 But while your servant was busy here and there, the man disappeared.” 
\b
\m And \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* said to him, “So shall your judgment be; you have pronounced it on yourself.” 
\b
\m 
\v 41 Then the prophet quickly removed the bandage from his eyes, and \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* recognized him as one of the prophets. 
\b
\m 
\v 42 And the prophet said to \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*, “This is what the LORD says: ‘Because you have let slip from your hand \zwd the man I had devoted to destruction|Q717814\zwd*,\f + \fr 20:42 \ft Forms of the Hebrew cherem refer to the giving over of things or persons to the LORD, either by destroying them or by giving them as an offering.\f* your life will be exchanged for his life, and your people for his people.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 43 Sullen and angry, \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* went home to Samaria. 
\c 21
\s1 Naboth’s Vineyard
\m 
\v 1 Some time after these events, \zwd Naboth the Jezreelite|Q44203\zwd* owned a vineyard in Jezreel next to the palace of \zwd Ahab king of Samaria|Q235901\zwd*. 
\v 2 So \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* said to \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd*, “Give me your vineyard to use as a vegetable garden, since it is next to my palace. I will give you a better vineyard in its place—or if you prefer, I will give you its value in silver.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 But \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* replied, “The LORD forbid that I should give you the inheritance of my fathers.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* went to his palace, sullen and angry because \zwd Naboth the Jezreelite|Q44203\zwd* had told him, “I will not give you the inheritance of my fathers.” He lay down on his bed, turned his face away, and refused to eat. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Soon \zwd his wife Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* came in and asked, “Why are you so sullen that you refuse to eat?” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* answered, “Because I spoke to \zwd Naboth the Jezreelite|Q44203\zwd* and told him, ‘Give me your vineyard for silver, or if you wish, I will give you another vineyard in its place.’ And he replied, ‘I will not give you my vineyard!’” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 But \zwd his wife Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* said to him, “Do you not reign over Israel? Get up, eat some food, and be cheerful, for I will get you the vineyard of \zwd Naboth the Jezreelite|Q44203\zwd*.” 
\s1 Jezebel’s Plot
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* wrote letters in \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*’s name, sealed them with his seal, and sent them to the elders and nobles who lived with \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* in his city. 
\v 9 In the letters she wrote: 
\b
\pmo “Proclaim a fast and give \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* a seat of honor among the people. 
\v 10 But seat two scoundrels opposite him and have them testify, ‘You have cursed both God and \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*!’ Then take him out and stone him to death.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 So the elders and nobles who lived in \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd*’s city did as \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* had instructed in the letters she had written to them. 
\v 12 They proclaimed a fast and gave \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* a seat of honor among the people. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And the two scoundrels came in and sat opposite \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd*, and these men testified against him before the people, saying, “\zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* has cursed both God and \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*!” 
\b
\m So they took him outside the city and stoned him to death. 
\v 14 Then they sent word to \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd*: “\zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* has been stoned to death.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* heard that \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* had been stoned to death, she said to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*, “Get up and take possession of the vineyard of \zwd Naboth the Jezreelite|Q44203\zwd*, who refused to give it to you for silver. For \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* is no longer alive, but dead.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 And when \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* heard that \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd* was dead, he got up and went down to take possession of the vineyard of \zwd Naboth the Jezreelite|Q44203\zwd*. 
\s1 Elijah Denounces Ahab and Jezebel
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then the word of the LORD came to \zwd Elijah the Tishbite|Q133507\zwd*, saying, 
\v 18 “Get up and go down to meet \zwd Ahab king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*, who is in Samaria. See, he is in the vineyard of \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd*, where he has gone to take possession of it. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Tell him that this is what the LORD says: ‘Have you not murdered a man and seized his land?’ 
\b
\m Then tell him that this is also what the LORD says: ‘In the place where the dogs licked up the blood of \zwd Naboth|Q44203\zwd*, there also the dogs will lick up your blood—yes, yours!’\f + \fr 21:19 \ft See 1 Kings 22:38\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 When \zwd Elijah|Q133507\zwd* arrived, \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* said to him, “So you have found me out, my enemy.” 
\b
\m He replied, “I have found you out because you have sold yourself to do evil in the sight of the LORD. 
\v 21 This is what the LORD says:\f + \fr 21:21 \ft See LXX; the source of the quotation is clarified in verses 17 and 23; Hebrew Behold.\f* 
\b
\q1 ‘I will bring calamity on you 
\q2 and consume your descendants; 
\q1 I will cut off from \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* every male in Israel, 
\q2 both slave and free. 
\q1 
\v 22 I will make your house like that of \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd* 
\q2 and like that of \zwd Baasha son of \zwd Ahijah|id="Q114841071" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q313224" t="2"\zwd*, 
\q1 because you have provoked My anger 
\q2 and caused Israel to sin.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 23 And the LORD also speaks concerning \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd*: 
\b
\q1 ‘The dogs will devour \zwd Jezebel|Q721295\zwd* 
\q2 by the wall of Jezreel.’\f + \fr 21:23 \ft Most Hebrew manuscripts; a few Hebrew manuscripts, Vulgate, and Syriac (see also 2 Kings 9:36) ‘The dogs will devour Jezebel at the plot of ground at Jezreel.’\f* 
\b
\q1 
\v 24 Anyone belonging to \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* who dies in the city 
\q2 will be eaten by dogs, 
\q1 and anyone who dies in the field 
\q2 will be eaten by the birds of the air.” 
\s1 Ahab’s Repentance
\b
\m 
\v 25 (Surely there was never one like \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*, who sold himself to do evil in the sight of the LORD, incited by \zwd his wife Jezebel|Q721295\zwd*. 
\v 26 He committed the most detestable acts by going after idols, just like the Amorites whom the LORD had driven out before the Israelites.) 
\b
\m 
\v 27 When \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* heard these words, he tore his clothes, put on sackcloth, and fasted. He lay down in sackcloth and walked around meekly. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Then the word of the LORD came to \zwd Elijah the Tishbite|Q133507\zwd*, saying: 
\v 29 “Have you seen how \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* has humbled himself before Me? Because he has humbled himself before Me, I will not bring the calamity during his days, but I will bring it upon his house in the days of \zwd his son|\zwd*.” 
\c 22
\s1 Ahab and the False Prophets
\r (2 Chronicles 18:1–11)
\m 
\v 1 Then three years passed without war between Aram and Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 However, in the third year, \zwd Jehoshaphat king of Judah|Q126689\zwd* went down to visit \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*, 
\v 3 who said to his servants, “Do you not know that Ramoth-gilead is ours, but we have failed to take it from the hand of \zwd the king of Aram|Q717814\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So he asked \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd*, “Will you go with me to fight against Ramoth-gilead?” 
\b
\m \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* answered \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*, “I am as you are, my people are your people, and my horses are your horses.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 But \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* also said to \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*, “Please inquire first for the word of the LORD.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* assembled the prophets, about four hundred men, and asked them, “Should I go to war against Ramoth-gilead, or should I refrain?” 
\b
\m “Go up,” they replied, “and the Lord will deliver it into the hand of \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 But \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* asked, “Is there not still a prophet of the LORD here of whom we can inquire?” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zwd The king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* answered, “There is still one man through whom we can inquire of the LORD, but I hate him because he never prophesies anything good for me, but only bad. He is \zwd Micaiah son of \zwd Imlah|\zwd*|Q1531578\zwd*.” 
\b
\m “\zwd The king|Q235901\zwd* should not say that!” \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* called one of his officials and said, “Bring \zwd Micaiah son of \zwd Imlah|\zwd*|Q1531578\zwd* at once.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Dressed in royal attire, \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* and \zwd Jehoshaphat king of Judah|Q126689\zwd* were sitting on their thrones at the threshing floor by the entrance of the gate of Samaria, with all the prophets prophesying before them. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Now \zwd Zedekiah son of \zwd Chenaanah|id="Q124790113" t="2"\zwd*|Q6586959\zwd* had made for himself iron horns and declared, “This is what the LORD says: ‘With these you shall gore the Arameans until they are finished off.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 And all the prophets were prophesying the same, saying, “Go up to Ramoth-gilead and triumph, for the LORD will deliver it into the hand of \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*.” 
\s1 Micaiah Prophesies against Ahab
\r (2 Chronicles 18:12–27)
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then the messenger who had gone to call \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* instructed him, “Behold now, with one accord the words of the prophets are favorable to \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*. So please let your words be like theirs, and speak favorably.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 But \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* said, “As surely as the LORD lives, I will speak whatever the LORD tells me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* arrived, \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd* asked him, “\zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd*, should we go to war against Ramoth-gilead, or should we refrain?” 
\b
\m “Go up and triumph,” \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* replied, “for the LORD will deliver it into the hand of \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 But \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd* said to him, “How many times must I make you swear not to tell me anything but the truth in the name of the LORD?” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* declared: 
\b
\q1 “I saw all Israel scattered on the hills 
\q2 like sheep without a shepherd. 
\q1 And the LORD said, ‘These people have no master; 
\q2 let each one return home in peace.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* said to \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd*, “Did I not tell you that he never prophesies good for me, but only bad?” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* continued, “Therefore hear the word of the LORD: I saw the LORD sitting on His throne, and all the host of heaven standing by Him on His right and on His left. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And the LORD said, ‘Who will entice \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* to march up and fall at Ramoth-gilead?’ 
\b
\m And one suggested this, and another that. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then a spirit came forward, stood before the LORD, and said, ‘I will entice him.’\f + \fr 22:21 \ft Some texts break verse 21 and begin verse 22 at this point.\f* 
\b
\m ‘By what means?’ asked the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And he replied, ‘I will go out and be a lying spirit in the mouths of all his prophets.’ 
\b
\m ‘You will surely entice him and prevail,’ said the LORD. ‘Go and do it.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So you see, the LORD has put a lying spirit in the mouths of all these prophets of yours, and the LORD has pronounced disaster against you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \zwd Zedekiah son of \zwd Chenaanah|id="Q124790113" t="2"\zwd*|Q6586959\zwd* went up, struck \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* in the face, and demanded, “Which way did the Spirit of the LORD go when He departed from me to speak with you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* replied, “You will soon see, on that day when you go and hide in an inner room.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 And \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* declared, “Take \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* and return him to \zwd Amon the governor of the city|Q107646275\zwd* and to \zwd Joash \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd*’s son|\zwd*, 
\v 27 and tell them that this is what \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd* says: ‘Put \zwd this man|Q1531578\zwd* in prison and feed him only bread and water until I return safely.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 But \zwd Micaiah|Q1531578\zwd* replied, “If you ever return safely, the LORD has not spoken through me.” Then he added, “Take heed, all you people!” 
\s1 Ahab’s Defeat and Death
\r (2 Chronicles 18:28–34)
\b
\m 
\v 29 \zdate|Q235901:P39:Q43459871:P582\*So \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* and \zwd Jehoshaphat king of Judah|Q126689\zwd* went up to Ramoth-gilead. 
\v 30 And \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* said to \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd*, “I will disguise myself and go into battle, but you wear your royal robes.” So \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* disguised himself and went into battle. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Now \zwd the king of Aram|Q717814\zwd* had ordered his thirty-two chariot commanders, “Do not fight with anyone, small or great, except \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 When the chariot commanders saw \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd*, they said, “Surely this is \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*!” So they turned to fight against him, but \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* cried out. 
\v 33 And when the chariot commanders saw that he was not \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*, they turned back from pursuing him. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 However, a certain man drew his bow without taking special aim, and he struck \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd* between the joints of his armor. So \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd* said to his charioteer, “Turn around \f + \fr 22:34 \ft Literally Turn your hand\f* and take me out of the battle, for I am badly wounded!” 
\b
\m 
\v 35 The battle raged throughout that day, and \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd* was propped up in his chariot facing the Arameans. And the blood from his wound ran out onto the floor of the chariot, and that evening he died. 
\v 36 As the sun was setting, the cry rang out in the army: 
\b
\q1 “Every man to his own city, 
\q2 and every man to his own land!” 
\b
\m 
\v 37 So \zwd the king|Q235901\zwd* died and was brought to Samaria, where they buried him. 
\v 38 And the chariot was washed at the pool of Samaria where the prostitutes bathed,\f + \fr 22:38 \ft Or the pool of Samaria, where they cleaned the weapons\f* and the dogs licked up \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*’s blood, according to the word that the LORD had spoken.\f + \fr 22:38 \ft See 1 Kings 21:19.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 39 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*, along with all his accomplishments and the ivory palace and all the cities he built, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\b
\m 
\v 40 And \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd* rested with his fathers, and \zwd his son Ahaziah|Q313213\zwd* reigned in his place.\zdate|Q313213:P39:Q43459871:P580\* 
\s1 Jehoshaphat Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 20:31–34)
\b
\m 
\v 41 \zdate|Q235901:P39:Q43459871:P580+P4Y\*In the fourth year of \zwd Ahab|Q235901\zwd*’s reign over Israel, \zwd Jehoshaphat son of \zwd Asa|id="Q313415" t="2"\zwd*|Q126689\zwd* became king of Judah. 
\v 42 \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* was thirty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem twenty-five years. His mother’s name was \zwd Azubah daughter of \zwd Shilhi|id="Q133454594" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q4833002" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 43 And \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* walked in all the ways of \zwd his father Asa|id="Q313415" t="2"\zwd*; he did not turn away from them, but did what was right in the eyes of the LORD. 
\b
\m The high places, however, were not removed; the people still sacrificed and burned incense on the high places. 
\v 44 \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* also made peace with \zwd the king of Israel|Q235901\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 45 As for the rest of the acts of \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd*, along with the might he exercised and how he waged war, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\v 46 He banished from the land the male shrine prostitutes who remained from the days of \zwd his father Asa|id="Q313415" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 47 And there was no king in Edom; a deputy served as king. 
\b
\m 
\v 48 \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* built ships of Tarshish \f + \fr 22:48 \ft Or a fleet of trading ships\f* to go to Ophir for gold, but they never set sail, because they were wrecked at Ezion-geber. 
\v 49 At that time \zwd Ahaziah son of \zwd Ahab|id="Q235901" t="2"\zwd*|Q313213\zwd* said to \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd*, “Let my servants sail with your servants,” but \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* refused. 
\b
\m 
\v 50 And \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd* rested with his fathers and was buried with them in the city of \zwd his father David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. And \zwd his son Jehoram|Q313215\zwd* reigned in his place.\zdate|Q313215:P39:Q10550167:P580+P4Y\* 
\s1 Ahaziah Reigns in Israel
\r (2 Kings 1:1–16)
\b
\m 
\v 51 \zdate|Q313213:P39:Q26835654:P580\*In the seventeenth year of \zwd Jehoshaphat|Q126689\zwd*’s reign over Judah, \zwd Ahaziah son of \zwd Ahab|id="Q235901" t="2"\zwd*|Q313213\zwd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria two years. 
\v 52 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD and walked in the ways of \zwd his father and mother|id="Q235901,Q721295" t="2"\zwd* and of \zwd Jeroboam son of \zwd Nebat|id="Q26835883" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q313219" t="2"\zwd*, who had caused Israel to sin. 
\b
\m 
\v 53 \zwd Ahaziah|Q313213\zwd* served and worshiped Baal, provoking the LORD, the God of Israel, to anger, just as \zwd his father|id="Q235901" t="2"\zwd* had done. 
